1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:26,710
listen practice speak practice makes you perfect have you ever found yourself um maybe in the office wanting to connect with colleagues or perhaps you know gently steer a conversation to its close but felt a little unsure about the best way to do it in english

2
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:27,700
Oh, absolutely.

3
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:33,450
It's a common scenario, I think, for many of us navigating those everyday workplace interactions.

4
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:41,280
Let's talk about those initial moments, those simple but actually quite crucial ways to break the ice or just check in with someone.

5
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:49,430
Our first approach is that classic how's it been or how's it going structure, starting with the present perfect tense.

6
00:00:49,690 --> 00:00:54,100
Yeah, that's a great starting point because it's so incredibly common when we ask, how has your day been?

7
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,820
Or maybe, how's your week been?

8
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:58,220
Or naturally using the present perfect tense.

9
00:00:58,300 --> 00:00:59,060
Right, has been.

10
00:00:59,350 --> 00:00:59,810
Exactly.

11
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:03,510
Because the period we're asking about, the work day or the week, it's still ongoing.

12
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:07,190
For instance, if it's, say, 3 p.m., the day isn't over yet.

13
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:07,740
Still happening.

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:08,620
Still happening.

15
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,660
Or if it's Wednesday, the week hasn't concluded.

16
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:17,190
So how has something been isn't asking about a finished event.

17
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:20,110
It focuses on how that time has progressed.

18
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,010
Well, up until this very moment, you're asking.

19
00:01:22,110 --> 00:01:22,430
OK.

20
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:27,800
It's a general inquiry, really, about their recent experiences and how things have unfolded so far.

21
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:34,890
And the subtle power of this question, I think, is that it asks for general updates without being too probing.

22
00:01:35,030 --> 00:01:35,790
Yeah, it's low pressure.

23
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:38,650
It's a low pressure way to initiate contact.

24
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:41,080
And you can easily vary it, too, can't you?

25
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:44,760
Instead of just day or week, you could ask, how's your morning been so far?

26
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,360
Adding the so far.

27
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:52,990
Adding so far really emphasizes that until now aspect, which is perfect for those morning interactions, maybe before lunch.

28
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:53,870
Definitely.

29
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:57,640
Or for something more work specific, you might ask, how's your new project been?

30
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:00,280
And this isn't just polite small talk, is it?

31
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:01,840
No, it's more strategic than that.

32
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:11,810
It feels like a strategic opener that signals you're a team player, maybe subtly inviting collaboration or even a request for help without putting immediate pressure on them.

33
00:02:11,790 --> 00:02:12,770
That's a really good point.

34
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:19,360
It fosters a sense of shared responsibility perhaps and shows genuine interest in their work, not just passing the time.

35
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:20,860
So what about replies?

36
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:22,830
What do people usually say?

37
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:25,300
Well, when it comes to replies, you'll hear a range.

38
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:32,500
A very, very common British answer, often paired with a little shrug, is not too bad.

39
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:33,250
Ah, yes.

40
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:34,710
The classic not too bad.

41
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:35,070
It is.

42
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:43,840
Now, for our American listeners, this might sound a bit understated, maybe even a little unenthusiastic if you're expecting a more, you know, effusive, it's going great.

43
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:44,300
Right.

44
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:46,640
Americans tend to be a bit more upbeat sometimes.

45
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:47,580
Sometimes, yeah.

46
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:49,730
But in British English, not too bad.

47
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:51,950
Often implies things are perfectly okay.

48
00:02:52,290 --> 00:02:55,800
You know, nothing exceptional maybe, but certainly not problematic.

49
00:02:55,860 --> 00:02:58,490
It's more of a neutral to positive response, actually.

50
00:02:58,640 --> 00:02:59,220
Interesting.

51
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,760
So not too bad is actually pretty good.

52
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:02,720
Often, yes.

53
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:03,300
Yeah.

54
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:04,140
Or at least acceptable.

55
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,970
You might also hear, it's been busy, which is very common everywhere.

56
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:08,530
Oh, yeah.

57
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:09,230
Everyone's busy.

58
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:10,350
Or just, it's been fine.

59
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:17,290
Now, what's fascinating here, and you mentioned this earlier, is the common native speaker tendency to shorten these replies.

60
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:18,230
Ah, yes.

61
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:26,920
So instead of the full present perfect, it has been busy, you'll frequently just hear busy or fine.

62
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:27,860
Just the adjective.

63
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:29,060
Just the adjective.

64
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:34,050
And this is perfectly casual and totally acceptable in a workplace setting.

65
00:03:34,730 --> 00:03:38,500
Doesn't sound rude or grammatically incorrect in a conversational context at all.

66
00:03:38,690 --> 00:03:48,830
That shortening is a brilliant point because it really highlights how native speakers often prioritize flow and, well, naturalness over sticking to strict grammatical rule.

67
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:49,450
Exactly.

68
00:03:49,690 --> 00:03:50,650
Makes it feel authentic.

69
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:53,810
It makes the conversation feel more authentic and less formal.

70
00:03:53,930 --> 00:03:54,610
Definitely.

71
00:03:54,590 --> 00:03:57,800
And then I suppose there are slightly more elaborate replies, too.

72
00:03:57,820 --> 00:03:58,280
There are.

73
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:00,060
People might say, it's been good.

74
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,220
Or maybe things have been good.

75
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:05,650
Now, when someone says things in this context.

76
00:04:05,690 --> 00:04:06,790
What does things mean?

77
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:08,110
Yeah, good question.

78
00:04:08,130 --> 00:04:14,310
They're often referring to general life events, you know, updates, news, or just general happenings in their world.

79
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:16,840
It's quite vague, but covers a lot.

80
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:19,000
So the new project's been good is direct.

81
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:19,640
Very direct.

82
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:22,200
But things have been good is broader.

83
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:22,890
Exactly.

84
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:25,860
It covers a broader sense of their well-being or situation.

85
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:33,870
And honestly, this how's it been approach is such a low pressure, widely accepted way to show basic interest.

86
00:04:34,450 --> 00:04:37,330
It's like a polite daily greeting that just goes one step further.

87
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:38,170
That's a friendly tone.

88
00:04:38,270 --> 00:04:45,980
Yeah, it says a friendly tone without demanding a deep, involved conversation, which, let's face it, is truly ideal for the busy workplace.

89
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:46,600
Definitely.

90
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:53,930
But what if you're specifically asking about something that's currently in progress right now rather than something that's been happening up until now?

91
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:55,150
Is there a difference?

92
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:57,460
Yes, there is a subtle difference.

93
00:04:57,620 --> 00:04:59,800
And that's where our second conversation starter comes in.

94
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:02,390
This one uses the present continuous tense.

95
00:05:02,930 --> 00:05:05,050
How's your day work role project trading going?

96
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:07,070
Ah, the ing form.

97
00:05:07,170 --> 00:05:07,650
Exactly.

98
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:12,620
Here, the focus is on the continuous nature of the current state or activity.

99
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:13,600
How is your day going?

100
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:18,530
Is asking about something that is actively unfolding or perhaps has been in progress over the last few hours.

101
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:19,830
So more immediate.

102
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:20,990
A bit more immediate, yeah.

103
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:21,710
Yeah.

104
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:30,560
It's a nice, easy way to inquire about a colleague's day at work, whether you're catching them in the middle of a task or just passing by their desk.

105
00:05:30,550 --> 00:05:39,020
So if I catch someone on their way to a meeting, maybe, or they're at their desk clearly deep in thought, I might choose, how's it going?

106
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,920
Rather than, how's it been?

107
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:41,860
You could, yeah.

108
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,660
It feels a bit more immediate, doesn't it?

109
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:44,660
Precisely.

110
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:47,310
It implies a sense of ongoing action.

111
00:05:47,890 --> 00:05:51,750
And you can adapt this endlessly, making it more specific, just like the first one.

112
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:52,050
Right.

113
00:05:52,410 --> 00:05:54,600
So how's your work going?

114
00:05:54,570 --> 00:05:57,580
is a general inquiry about their current tasks.

115
00:05:58,280 --> 00:05:59,440
How's your new role going?

116
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:06,570
It's perfect for a colleague who has recently taken on a new position or title within the company.

117
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:08,190
It doesn't have to be a new job entirely.

118
00:06:08,290 --> 00:06:09,810
Just a new responsibility, maybe?

119
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:10,450
Exactly.

120
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:13,180
Or you could ask, how's your new project going?

121
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:19,920
If you know they're really engrossed in a significant undertaking, or perhaps, how's your training going?

122
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:24,900
If they're undergoing some new learning program, it shows you're aware of their current activities.

123
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:25,900
You're paying attention.

124
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,240
You're paying attention, yeah.

125
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:27,520
Okay.

126
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:35,190
And when it comes to replies for how's it going, the classic grammatically precise response.

127
00:06:35,250 --> 00:06:39,780
Because the question starts with how would involve an adverb, right?

128
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,540
That's technically correct, yes.

129
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:45,630
So it's going well, or maybe it's going quite smoothly.

130
00:06:46,090 --> 00:06:54,350
But, just like we discussed earlier, there's often a more casual, extremely common reply you'll hear, particularly in American English.

131
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:55,510
It's going good.

132
00:06:55,530 --> 00:06:56,710
Ah, going good.

133
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:57,670
You hear that all the time.

134
00:06:57,750 --> 00:06:58,430
All the time.

135
00:06:58,410 --> 00:07:04,340
Even though, you know, technically good is an adjective and well is the adverb we should use with going.

136
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:11,830
The phrase it's going good is so widely used that it's really not considered rude or wrong in most professional casual settings.

137
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:13,810
No, it just sounds very natural and friendly.

138
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:14,310
It does.

139
00:07:14,530 --> 00:07:19,900
It's important for our listeners to recognize this common usage and not perhaps judge it too harshly grammatically.

140
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:20,460
Good point.

141
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:23,200
It's about understanding how people actually speak.

142
00:07:23,190 --> 00:07:23,990
That's spot on.

143
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:26,910
It's a perfect example of spoken language evolving, really.

144
00:07:27,250 --> 00:07:32,470
Then you have replies that are a bit more neutral or even subtly negative, like, it's going okay.

145
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:32,750
Okay.

146
00:07:32,850 --> 00:07:33,810
The classic okay.

147
00:07:34,170 --> 00:07:39,300
We often use okay to imply things aren't amazing, but they're manageable.

148
00:07:39,870 --> 00:07:39,940
Bye.

149
00:07:39,990 --> 00:07:42,400
Think of it as that polite 5 out of 10 we mentioned.

150
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:42,720
Right.

151
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:44,420
Not great, not terrible.

152
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:45,120
Exactly.

153
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:52,230
Or if they're making progress, but it's maybe not particularly fast or easy, they might say, it's coming along.

154
00:07:52,390 --> 00:07:52,890
Coming along.

155
00:07:53,050 --> 00:07:57,620
Meaning it's progressing steadily, perhaps, but maybe not quickly or without hurdles.

156
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:01,760
A common scenario might be, how's that new software integration going?

157
00:08:01,990 --> 00:08:04,170
And the reply, ah, it's coming along.

158
00:08:04,330 --> 00:08:05,730
Still a few bugs to iron out.

159
00:08:05,710 --> 00:08:09,020
OK, signals progress, but hints at challenges.

160
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:09,840
Precisely.

161
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:14,740
And for something really challenging, you might hear the new training is tough, but we're getting through it.

162
00:08:14,890 --> 00:08:15,740
Getting through it.

163
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:18,560
Which means they're persevering, taking it step by step.

164
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:21,370
It's a great insight into their resilience, actually.

165
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:22,400
Yeah, that's true.

166
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:30,520
So this phrase, how's it going, seems ideal for checking in on specific ongoing tasks or maybe transitions your colleague might be experiencing.

167
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:36,830
It demonstrates empathy, I think, and a willingness to listen, which can really strengthen those professional bonds.

168
00:08:37,410 --> 00:08:38,740
It's practical small talk.

169
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:39,220
It is.

170
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:46,370
It shows you're engaged, and it allows you to offer support, perhaps, or simply acknowledge their efforts without demanding a long story.

171
00:08:46,550 --> 00:08:47,890
Okay, let's broaden the scope now.

172
00:08:48,290 --> 00:08:53,120
Our third conversation starter uses the phrase, how are things going with?

173
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:54,620
This sounds incredibly versatile.

174
00:08:54,640 --> 00:09:05,730
It really is, because it allows you to inquire about people like teams, clients, bosses, or significant work-related things like, say, projects or even entire departments.

175
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:07,630
So things is the key word here.

176
00:09:07,910 --> 00:09:09,780
Things is the flexible part, yes.

177
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:18,700
This question seeks updates on how situations are unfolding, and crucially, it subtly implies an offer of help or maybe advice if needed.

178
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:20,160
It's not just asking.

179
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:22,120
It's showing willingness to engage.

180
00:09:22,170 --> 00:09:22,550
Right.

181
00:09:22,630 --> 00:09:24,830
So you could ask, how are things going with your team?

182
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:29,120
Which is a collective inquiry about their group dynamics or maybe current workload.

183
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:29,700
Exactly.

184
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:31,700
Or how are things going with your new client?

185
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:33,890
If you know they've recently taken on a new account.

186
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:37,890
It's also perfectly appropriate for asking about a colleague's superior.

187
00:09:38,510 --> 00:09:39,890
How are things going with your new boss?

188
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:43,540
And using things makes it feel less personal, perhaps.

189
00:09:43,670 --> 00:09:44,190
I think so.

190
00:09:45,070 --> 00:09:58,650
This phrase subtly shifts the burden from the individual colleague to the situation or the things, making it potentially easier for colleagues to open up about challenges without feeling like they're just complaining personally.

191
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:01,080
That's a clever psychological aspect.

192
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:01,720
It can be.

193
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:02,300
Yeah.

194
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:08,090
It's an empathetic invitation to maybe problem solve together, not just a casual check in like, how are you?

195
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:10,060
And you mentioned the flexibility of things.

196
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:16,180
I know I often hear people who are learning English try to translate directly and say something like, how's your boss going?

197
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:16,440
Yes.

198
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:18,780
That sounds quite odd in English, doesn't it?

199
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:19,350
It does.

200
00:10:19,410 --> 00:10:22,270
Like you're asking if the boss is physically moving somewhere.

201
00:10:22,290 --> 00:10:22,730
Right.

202
00:10:23,130 --> 00:10:25,960
But how are things going with your boss?

203
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:31,150
Is perfectly natural because things makes it flexible for both objects and people.

204
00:10:31,170 --> 00:10:32,420
You can use it for almost anything.

205
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:33,420
Like new hires.

206
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:34,180
Absolutely.

207
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:34,600
Yeah.

208
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:38,830
How are things going with the new guy or even a new department that's been recently restructured?

209
00:10:39,030 --> 00:10:39,190
Yeah.

210
00:10:39,210 --> 00:10:41,300
How are things going with the marketing integration?

211
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:41,660
Wow.

212
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:42,160
Okay.

213
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:44,300
And could it extend beyond work?

214
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:53,070
If you have a close enough relationship, and maybe you're outside of the immediate work context, like grabbing coffee, it could potentially venture into personal life.

215
00:10:53,590 --> 00:10:54,970
How are things going with your family?

216
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:59,200
Or even just a very general, how are things going, as an inquiry about life overall.

217
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:01,200
But tread carefully there.

218
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:02,060
Good advice.

219
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:04,620
Stick to work topics unless you know them well.

220
00:11:05,390 --> 00:11:06,750
Generally safer, yes.

221
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:14,640
We're asking for these updates primarily because we want to know if situations are progressing well, just okay, or perhaps not so great.

222
00:11:15,490 --> 00:11:22,950
And critically, if there are problems, it really opens the door for us to potentially offer assistance or simply lend an ear.

223
00:11:23,030 --> 00:11:25,450
It's polite, appropriate, yet friendly.

224
00:11:25,770 --> 00:11:26,410
Exactly.

225
00:11:27,050 --> 00:11:29,300
It shows you care and are willing to support.

226
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:39,210
This question subtly creates an opening for problem solving or collaboration, making small talk potentially more meaningful by showing genuine interest in a colleague's challenges and successes.

227
00:11:39,470 --> 00:11:42,220
Now, a listener might be wondering, what if I'm speaking to someone more senior?

228
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:44,800
How are things going with still work?

229
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:46,320
Is it respectful enough?

230
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:47,710
That's a great practical question.

231
00:11:48,150 --> 00:11:50,880
And in most professional settings, yes, it absolutely does work.

232
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:55,530
It's generally considered respectful, and it shows you're observant of their broader responsibilities.

233
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:59,870
So if I asked you, maybe, how's everything going with your new team launch?

234
00:12:00,350 --> 00:12:02,990
I might reply, oh, it's going well, thanks for asking.

235
00:12:03,370 --> 00:12:05,860
Or, it's going smoothly, mostly.

236
00:12:06,030 --> 00:12:06,340
Yeah.

237
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:17,710
And just like with the previous phrases, the casual, it's going good, or it's going great, are also perfectly acceptable and very common responses, especially in American English.

238
00:12:17,690 --> 00:12:19,830
Still hearing going good a lot.

239
00:12:19,930 --> 00:12:20,310
You will.

240
00:12:21,050 --> 00:12:26,220
You can also use the present perfect here, too, because the work or situation is likely still ongoing.

241
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:30,690
So to how are things going with your new boss?

242
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:32,070
Someone might reply.

243
00:12:32,630 --> 00:12:35,390
It's been a bit tricky so far, but we're finding our rhythm.

244
00:12:35,510 --> 00:12:35,820
OK.

245
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:37,200
Honest but professional.

246
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:37,800
Exactly.

247
00:12:38,240 --> 00:12:40,300
Or it's been pretty good so far, actually.

248
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:42,950
We've been getting to know each other and establishing new workflows.

249
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:51,120
Other positive responses could be things like we're making a lot of progress on the XYZ project or we're getting a lot done on the quarterly report.

250
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:52,750
Those sound very productive.

251
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:55,590
They convey a clear sense of achievement and forward movement.

252
00:12:55,790 --> 00:12:56,030
Yeah.

253
00:12:56,130 --> 00:13:02,100
And for those neutral or slightly negative replies, are we back to, they're going okay?

254
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:03,520
We often are, yes.

255
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:10,930
Again, it's that polite 5 out of 10, a way to signal things aren't perfect without launching into a complaint session.

256
00:13:11,390 --> 00:13:13,800
But what if things are genuinely not going well?

257
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:14,640
You touched on this.

258
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:17,120
How much should one share in the workplace?

259
00:13:17,260 --> 00:13:21,980
That's such a crucial point, and it really depends on the culture of your workplace and your relationship with the person.

260
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:34,040
While you might hear someone say, perhaps to a trusted colleague, things aren't going that well, or honestly, things haven't been great, it's vital to consider the appropriate level of sharing negativity.

261
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:35,820
You don't want to be the office complainer.

262
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:36,900
Exactly.

263
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:46,540
You don't want to gain a reputation at work for always complaining or moaning about things, as that can really down office morale and even affect how you're perceived professionally.

264
00:13:46,720 --> 00:13:49,510
So things are going okay might be the safest bet.

265
00:13:49,610 --> 00:13:57,010
Often, things are going okay is the most you'd want to share if you're not entirely happy, especially with someone you don't know extremely well.

266
00:13:57,730 --> 00:14:01,500
It gives a little hint without being overly negative or inviting too much probing.

267
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:06,020
It's about finding that balance between transparency and professionalism.

268
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:12,610
Yeah, the goal is often to invite support or understanding, not necessarily to just dump all your problems on someone.

269
00:14:12,930 --> 00:14:13,370
Precisely.

270
00:14:13,910 --> 00:14:15,830
This question, how are things going with?

271
00:14:17,010 --> 00:14:19,070
It's powerful because of its breadth.

272
00:14:19,050 --> 00:14:25,900
It allows you to engage meaningfully on various fronts, fostering deeper professional relationships by showing genuine interest.

273
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:32,290
It really broadens the scope of your small talk and can even lead to tangible problem-solving opportunities if used well.

274
00:14:32,670 --> 00:14:33,630
Okay, let's pivot now.

275
00:14:33,690 --> 00:14:34,590
We've talked about checking in.

276
00:14:34,650 --> 00:14:39,400
Now let's move to a crucial aspect of workplace interaction, asking for feedback and opinions.

277
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:46,210
We have a few different approaches here, starting with, how do you like the job working from home Teen Dynamics new role company project so far?

278
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:46,740
Right.

279
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:52,250
This phrase is specifically tailored for situations when something new has been introduced or experienced.

280
00:14:52,750 --> 00:15:01,940
It could be a brand new job, yes, but also a new company policy like working from home, a new project they've just started, or even changes in team structure or dynamics.

281
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:04,960
And the how at the beginning is key here, isn't it?

282
00:15:04,980 --> 00:15:06,050
Absolutely crucial.

283
00:15:06,070 --> 00:15:16,400
Because how do you like prompts for a detailed opinion, a description of their experience, not just a simple yes or no answer like, do you like it?

284
00:15:16,630 --> 00:15:16,950
Right.

285
00:15:16,970 --> 00:15:17,590
Do you like it?

286
00:15:17,970 --> 00:15:18,750
Gets a yes, no.

287
00:15:18,970 --> 00:15:19,560
How do you like it?

288
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:20,180
Needs more.

289
00:15:20,380 --> 00:15:20,900
Exactly.

290
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:27,490
And that little phrase, so far, at the end, grounds it firmly in the present perfect, meaning until now.

291
00:15:28,230 --> 00:15:34,180
It subtly indicates that you're asking for an ongoing evaluation, their impression up to this point, not necessarily a final judgment.

292
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:34,560
Got it.

293
00:15:34,660 --> 00:15:40,460
So if your company just introduced a new remote work scheme, you could ask a colleague, so how do you like working from home so far?

294
00:15:40,750 --> 00:15:41,440
Are you enjoying it?

295
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:42,200
Is it a bit weird?

296
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:43,140
Perfect example.

297
00:15:43,540 --> 00:15:47,030
Or if a new colleague just started maybe last week, how do you like the job so far?

298
00:15:47,230 --> 00:15:47,870
Has it been okay?

299
00:15:47,890 --> 00:15:48,730
Is it what you expected?

300
00:15:49,030 --> 00:15:51,390
You're looking for more than just, yes, it's fine.

301
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:55,140
You're inviting them to share their actual experiences and observations.

302
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:55,640
You are.

303
00:15:55,860 --> 00:15:57,710
And an interesting point about replies.

304
00:15:58,730 --> 00:16:08,950
When you hear someone start their reply with, yeah, like, yeah, it's been really great, that yeah often isn't a direct answer to a hidden yes-no question.

305
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:09,850
What is it then?

306
00:16:10,270 --> 00:16:20,910
It's often just a filler word, a sort of soft opening, a way of signaling they're about to speak and maybe gathering their thoughts before launching into their more detailed explanations.

307
00:16:20,920 --> 00:16:22,410
Ah, okay.

308
00:16:22,430 --> 00:16:24,090
Like a conversational throat clearing.

309
00:16:24,570 --> 00:16:25,170
Sort of, yes.

310
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:28,960
So typical replies might include, yeah, it's been really great so far.

311
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:32,570
I'm loving the flexibility or I've been enjoying it so far.

312
00:16:32,610 --> 00:16:34,010
The team has been very welcoming.

313
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:37,600
Another common and concise one is so far so good.

314
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:39,190
Meaning no problems yet.

315
00:16:39,170 --> 00:16:39,830
Exactly.

316
00:16:40,410 --> 00:16:41,870
Everything has been good up to this point.

317
00:16:42,230 --> 00:16:43,490
No major issues encountered.

318
00:16:43,850 --> 00:16:47,880
For more descriptive answers, you might hear things like, I really like the project itself.

319
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:52,000
It's challenging, but it's incredibly interesting, even with the tight deadlines.

320
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:53,100
Giving specifics.

321
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:53,960
Giving specifics.

322
00:16:54,230 --> 00:16:57,030
Or, honestly, it's been great so far.

323
00:16:57,510 --> 00:17:00,290
People are really friendly, and the training has been very eye-opening.

324
00:17:00,330 --> 00:17:01,710
I feel like I'm learning a lot.

325
00:17:01,950 --> 00:17:08,520
So this question is really your go-to when you want to extract more than a simple affirmative from a colleague.

326
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:09,190
Definitely.

327
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:16,320
It encourages them to share their experiences and observations, which provides a much richer conversational exchange.

328
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:22,570
And it gives you opportunities maybe to offer advice or share your own similar experiences if appropriate.

329
00:17:22,690 --> 00:17:23,930
It opens a dialogue, yeah.

330
00:17:23,950 --> 00:17:25,470
It just gets a one-word answer.

331
00:17:25,510 --> 00:17:26,570
But what if they don't like it?

332
00:17:27,150 --> 00:17:31,090
How do people usually respond politely without being overly negative?

333
00:17:31,310 --> 00:17:32,510
That's a great practical point.

334
00:17:32,990 --> 00:17:42,650
If they're perhaps struggling a bit or finding it difficult, they might say something like, well, it's been a bit challenging, to be honest, or maybe I'm still getting used to it.

335
00:17:42,670 --> 00:17:44,270
OK, acknowledging the difficulty.

336
00:17:44,290 --> 00:17:44,510
Yes.

337
00:17:45,150 --> 00:17:47,930
And in this case, as the asker.

338
00:17:47,910 --> 00:17:50,820
You might offer a sympathetic response like, oh, I can imagine.

339
00:17:50,880 --> 00:17:51,980
Is there anything I can do to help?

340
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:55,790
Or simply, yeah, new things always take a bit of adjustment, don't they?

341
00:17:55,810 --> 00:17:56,890
What should you not do?

342
00:17:57,110 --> 00:18:02,460
Probably what you shouldn't do is immediately jump in with your own complaints or criticisms, even if you happen to share them.

343
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:09,570
It's generally better to listen and be supportive first before sharing your own perspective, especially if theirs is negative.

344
00:18:09,700 --> 00:18:10,300
Good advice.

345
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:14,370
Okay, building on that, let's consider a more casual opinion-seeking question.

346
00:18:14,810 --> 00:18:18,590
This one often comes up after a meeting or maybe when a new development is announced.

347
00:18:19,250 --> 00:18:24,320
What do you think about Sarah's idea, the boss's suggestion, the new scheduling merger, that email?

348
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:28,810
Yes, this is perfect for those informal but still professional discussions.

349
00:18:29,290 --> 00:18:35,380
It's about discussing ideas or potential decisions that have just come up, and you're seeking a colleague's perspective.

350
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:38,240
Do they agree, disagree, have alternatives?

351
00:18:38,590 --> 00:18:40,830
And importantly, this isn't office gossip, right?

352
00:18:41,430 --> 00:18:42,320
No, ideally not.

353
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:48,610
It should be a professional discussion aimed at understanding different viewpoints or gauging collective sentiment.

354
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:52,710
It helps everyone get on the same page or at least understand where others stand.

355
00:18:53,230 --> 00:18:56,660
So you might say after a meeting, what do you think about the boss's suggestion?

356
00:18:56,740 --> 00:18:58,540
It seemed quite ambitious, right?

357
00:18:58,600 --> 00:18:59,120
Exactly.

358
00:18:59,330 --> 00:19:02,410
Or maybe, what do you think about the new proposed schedule?

359
00:19:02,650 --> 00:19:04,210
I don't know, starting at 8 instead of 9.

360
00:19:04,390 --> 00:19:05,790
I'm not sure I'm a fan of that idea.

361
00:19:05,950 --> 00:19:08,640
Yeah, sharing a tentative opinion to invite theirs.

362
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:09,400
Precisely.

363
00:19:09,780 --> 00:19:14,590
You can even use it for significant company changes, like, what do you think about the merger rumors?

364
00:19:15,670 --> 00:19:17,190
Or maybe after an all-staff email.

365
00:19:17,690 --> 00:19:20,820
What did you think about that email they sent out regarding the new policy?

366
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:25,850
It's a good way to get a sense of how people are feeling, but keeping it professional.

367
00:19:25,970 --> 00:19:30,240
As you said, we often try to separate our personal feelings from our work analysis.

368
00:19:30,440 --> 00:19:30,950
We try.

369
00:19:31,050 --> 00:19:37,500
So you're looking for their professional opinion, ideally, not necessarily just an emotional gut reaction.

370
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:44,020
So replies could be things like, hmm, I think it makes sense given our current resources, or maybe more cautious.

371
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:48,150
I think it might work, but I'm not sure we have the bandwidth for it right now.

372
00:19:48,370 --> 00:19:48,770
Exactly.

373
00:19:49,290 --> 00:19:52,170
Or someone might say, well, I'm not totally convinced yet.

374
00:19:52,490 --> 00:19:55,000
I still have a few questions about the implementation plan.

375
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:56,860
Expressing reservations.

376
00:19:56,980 --> 00:19:57,200
Right.

377
00:19:57,540 --> 00:19:58,820
What about more general replies?

378
00:19:59,220 --> 00:20:06,070
You'll also hear more generic summary responses, especially if someone hasn't fully formed their opinion yet or wants to be diplomatic.

379
00:20:07,150 --> 00:20:12,650
Things like, I think overall it's probably a good idea, though it will require some adjustments.

380
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:15,680
Using overall or in general.

381
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:17,740
Yes, those are useful summary words here.

382
00:20:17,800 --> 00:20:24,410
Or conversely, I think overall it probably won't work well in our department just because of our unique workflow.

383
00:20:25,390 --> 00:20:28,910
Another very common reply is, you know, I'm not sure what I think yet.

384
00:20:28,950 --> 00:20:31,950
It seems to have pros and it definitely has cons.

385
00:20:32,470 --> 00:20:33,820
Acknowledging complexity.

386
00:20:33,950 --> 00:20:35,810
Right, acknowledging it's not black and white.

387
00:20:36,530 --> 00:20:41,200
Or if they feel it needs improvement, they might say, I think it could be better, perhaps with a more phased approach.

388
00:20:41,500 --> 00:20:46,630
Or I think the concept is okay, but the execution could use some work, especially around communication.

389
00:20:47,070 --> 00:20:53,170
This phrase seems perfect for those post-meeting debriefs or just casually discussing recent company announcements.

390
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:54,100
It really is.

391
00:20:54,120 --> 00:21:02,370
It allows you to gauge collective sentiment, share your own thoughts tentatively, and contribute to a shared understanding of new initiatives.

392
00:21:02,480 --> 00:21:07,210
It helps build that sense of team cohesion, knowing you're all processing the same information.

393
00:21:07,510 --> 00:21:10,380
Okay, now, what if you need a more formal opinion?

394
00:21:10,390 --> 00:21:17,120
Maybe you're actually in a meeting or writing a formal email or having a structured discussion where a more elaborate view is expected.

395
00:21:18,030 --> 00:21:18,490
Ah, yes.

396
00:21:19,430 --> 00:21:26,720
In that case, you'd likely elevate the language slightly and use something like, what are your thoughts on the new project presentation software of the matter?

397
00:21:26,930 --> 00:21:29,600
What are your thoughts on feels more serious?

398
00:21:29,780 --> 00:21:30,100
It is.

399
00:21:30,460 --> 00:21:35,830
This signals a significant shift in formality and seriousness compared to what do you think.

400
00:21:35,850 --> 00:21:45,700
When you ask what are your thoughts on, you're explicitly inviting someone to elaborate, to provide a longer, more detailed answer, perhaps with supporting examples or more in-depth analysis.

401
00:21:45,680 --> 00:21:47,950
It's an invitation to elaborate more.

402
00:21:47,990 --> 00:21:49,010
Exactly.

403
00:21:49,130 --> 00:21:54,600
It's an invitation to provide a well-considered viewpoint, not just an off-the-cuff reaction.

404
00:21:55,240 --> 00:22:09,080
So a boss might ask you this in a formal review meeting, or a senior colleague might ask if they genuinely want your professional, comprehensive opinion, perhaps for a joint report or a decision they need to make.

405
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:12,690
So if someone asks you this, you need to be prepared to talk more.

406
00:22:12,790 --> 00:22:13,390
Definitely.

407
00:22:13,970 --> 00:22:18,290
Be prepared to share quite a bit more, demonstrating your critical thinking and reasoning.

408
00:22:19,030 --> 00:22:22,420
You might hear it framed as, what are your thoughts on the new project proposal?

409
00:22:22,760 --> 00:22:24,560
I'd appreciate your detailed input.

410
00:22:24,580 --> 00:22:27,160
Or maybe, what are your thoughts on the presentation this morning?

411
00:22:27,220 --> 00:22:29,440
I'm keen to hear your professional opinion on the strategy.

412
00:22:29,560 --> 00:22:30,140
Precisely.

413
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:33,150
Or, what are your thoughts on the new software we rolled out?

414
00:22:33,650 --> 00:22:37,170
We're looking for constructive feedback on usability and integration.

415
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:41,130
And this is where that professional filter we talked about becomes even more important, right?

416
00:22:41,150 --> 00:22:41,910
Absolutely vital.

417
00:22:42,390 --> 00:22:51,660
Even if personally you say, hate the new software, maybe it's slow, the buttons are confusing, you want to just blurt that out in response to what are your thoughts?

418
00:22:51,840 --> 00:22:53,510
No, probably not constructive.

419
00:22:53,490 --> 00:22:54,750
Not very constructive, no.

420
00:22:55,010 --> 00:22:56,130
You'd frame it professionally.

421
00:22:56,430 --> 00:22:59,900
You might say something like, well, my initial thoughts are that it needs some work.

422
00:23:00,280 --> 00:23:06,230
There seem to be still some kinks to iron out, particularly regarding the integration with our existing systems.

423
00:23:06,930 --> 00:23:11,980
However, overall, I can certainly see the potential benefits and why we've installed it.

424
00:23:12,260 --> 00:23:15,550
Focusing on specifics and potential, not just negativity.

425
00:23:15,610 --> 00:23:16,130
Exactly.

426
00:23:16,690 --> 00:23:25,240
It's about expressing a constructive, professional viewpoint that ideally focuses on solutions or areas for improvement, not just problems.

427
00:23:25,460 --> 00:23:28,260
So the responses are likely to be more strategic.

428
00:23:28,480 --> 00:23:33,930
Yes, typically more strategic and often framed in terms of benefits, challenges, and perhaps next steps.

429
00:23:34,330 --> 00:23:41,720
For instance, a thoughtful reply might be, I can understand the benefits outlined on paper, especially the potential for increased efficiency.

430
00:23:41,780 --> 00:23:47,830
However, I still think there are a number of details we need to work out regarding resource allocation and the necessary training.

431
00:23:48,270 --> 00:23:49,090
Okay, balanced.

432
00:23:49,110 --> 00:23:49,510
Balanced.

433
00:23:49,990 --> 00:23:50,370
Or maybe.

434
00:23:50,830 --> 00:23:59,890
Personally, I think the project sounds quite promising conceptually, and perhaps we should explore it a little further, maybe by conducting a small pilot program first to test the waters.

435
00:24:00,330 --> 00:24:01,430
Suggesting a next step.

436
00:24:02,030 --> 00:24:02,510
Smart.

437
00:24:02,560 --> 00:24:02,860
Yes.

438
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:20,940
And if you genuinely need more time to gather your thoughts or data, a great strategic apply is something like, overall, I feel that we're probably heading in the right direction with this, but I'd really like to get back to you with some more specific thoughts or perhaps some concrete ideas after I've had a chance to review the detailed data.

439
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:26,930
Ah, that's a fantastic way to buy time while still providing a preliminary, generally positive answer.

440
00:24:27,110 --> 00:24:27,390
It is.

441
00:24:27,750 --> 00:24:33,460
Or, if you have a strong, well-reasoned, negative professional opinion, you could phrase it carefully.

442
00:24:34,780 --> 00:24:42,290
Based on my analysis and my opinion, I don't currently see this working for us in the long run, primarily due to our existing infrastructure constraints.

443
00:24:42,990 --> 00:24:47,760
However, I would certainly like to discuss this further and present a deeper analysis of the potential risks involved.

444
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,080
Very professional, even when disagreeing.

445
00:24:50,180 --> 00:24:52,340
The key is being prepared and reasoned.

446
00:24:52,860 --> 00:24:54,360
When asked, what are your thoughts on?

447
00:24:55,180 --> 00:24:57,480
You're being invited to provide thoughtful analysis.

448
00:24:58,350 --> 00:25:06,790
It's a real opportunity to showcase your critical thinking and professional acumen, demonstrating that you can contribute beyond just surface level observations.

449
00:25:07,410 --> 00:25:10,280
You can definitely feel the language shift with this question.

450
00:25:10,740 --> 00:25:15,300
It's less easygoing, much more serious, and it really demands a well-reasoned response.

451
00:25:16,040 --> 00:25:18,780
Okay, let's move to our final conversation starter.

452
00:25:19,320 --> 00:25:21,640
This one, did you hear Reed see about?

453
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:23,850
Sounds a bit more casual again.

454
00:25:23,870 --> 00:25:24,370
It is, yes.

455
00:25:24,930 --> 00:25:32,940
This one is slightly more casual and absolutely perfect for those quick break room chats or maybe interactions as you grab a coffee or tea or just pass someone in the hallway.

456
00:25:33,500 --> 00:25:38,110
It's excellent for staying informed and connected without necessarily needing a lengthy dialogue.

457
00:25:38,270 --> 00:25:40,580
And you said this phrase serves two purposes.

458
00:25:40,740 --> 00:25:40,940
Yes.

459
00:25:41,500 --> 00:25:45,950
First, quite obviously, it checks the person is aware of a particular piece of news or information.

460
00:25:46,670 --> 00:25:52,770
But second, it implicitly and sometimes explicitly asks for their opinion or reaction to it.

461
00:25:53,130 --> 00:25:53,490
Oh, OK.

462
00:25:53,510 --> 00:25:55,990
So it's like, are you aware of X and what's your take on it?

463
00:25:56,090 --> 00:25:56,970
Pretty much, yeah.

464
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:09,430
It's a great way to engage with recent updates, especially if maybe an important email went out to everyone in the office, or if there's been some big company news that might change how people work or affect general morale.

465
00:26:10,350 --> 00:26:12,320
It taps into that shared context.

466
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:13,020
Shared knowledge.

467
00:26:13,060 --> 00:26:13,320
Yeah.

468
00:26:13,460 --> 00:26:20,470
And you'll notice we typically use three main verbs here, each with a slightly different nuance depending on how the information was likely received.

469
00:26:20,510 --> 00:26:20,610
Okay.

470
00:26:20,730 --> 00:26:21,470
What are they?

471
00:26:21,490 --> 00:26:23,560
Well, you have, did you hear about something?

472
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:29,950
This is common for news that spreads verbally or maybe announcements like, did you hear about the new regulations coming next month?

473
00:26:30,280 --> 00:26:34,140
Or did you hear about the potential layoffs in the other division?

474
00:26:34,630 --> 00:26:37,670
Or even good news, did you hear about the big contract we won?

475
00:26:37,970 --> 00:26:38,070
Okay.

476
00:26:38,410 --> 00:26:41,800
Hear about for spoken news or general topic?

477
00:26:42,060 --> 00:26:42,760
Generally, yes.

478
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:45,660
Then there's did you read about something?

479
00:26:45,820 --> 00:26:49,670
This obviously implies written information like an email, memo, or an article.

480
00:26:50,310 --> 00:26:51,890
Did you read about the new schedule changes?

481
00:26:52,290 --> 00:26:55,600
Or did you read about the company's quarterly results in the newsletter?

482
00:26:56,060 --> 00:26:58,440
Notice the preposition about is still used here.

483
00:26:58,660 --> 00:26:58,980
Got it.

484
00:26:59,160 --> 00:26:59,880
And the third one.

485
00:27:00,010 --> 00:27:02,180
The third one is, did you see something?

486
00:27:02,780 --> 00:27:11,670
Now, interestingly, we usually don't use a preposition like about with see when referring to a specific item you could visually look at.

487
00:27:12,050 --> 00:27:12,550
Oh, right.

488
00:27:12,590 --> 00:27:13,170
How does that work?

489
00:27:13,450 --> 00:27:15,990
So it's, did you see that message from the boss?

490
00:27:16,730 --> 00:27:20,930
Referring to the specific email, not, did you see about that message?

491
00:27:20,950 --> 00:27:23,500
Or did you see the new safety poster they put up?

492
00:27:24,340 --> 00:27:26,780
Or did you see the updated project timeline?

493
00:27:26,760 --> 00:27:32,490
So C for specific visual items, hear about or read about for topics or news.

494
00:27:32,930 --> 00:27:33,830
That's a good general rule.

495
00:27:33,890 --> 00:27:34,130
Yes.

496
00:27:34,290 --> 00:27:38,060
It's a subtle distinction, but it reflects natural English usage.

497
00:27:38,080 --> 00:27:43,830
You typically use C for something visual, a poster, a specific document, an email on a screen.

498
00:27:44,150 --> 00:27:47,450
OK. And you mentioned this might be related to office gossip.

499
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:49,380
Well, yes, in a way.

500
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:56,290
It's a prime question for what we might call office buzz or keeping up with the grapevine, but hopefully in a positive, lighthearted, professional context.

501
00:27:56,430 --> 00:27:57,590
Not malicious gossip.

502
00:27:57,730 --> 00:28:05,570
No, ideally not trying to spread harmful rumors, but rather participating in the natural flow of information and shared reactions within a team or the company.

503
00:28:06,090 --> 00:28:07,130
It's about staying connected.

504
00:28:07,490 --> 00:28:13,500
So if someone asks, maybe, did you hear about the new paperwork we need to do for expense claims now?

505
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:16,340
What are some common replies?

506
00:28:16,470 --> 00:28:21,570
Well, common affirmative replies would include, yeah, no, it looks like a lot of extra steps, doesn't it?

507
00:28:22,030 --> 00:28:23,000
Or, yeah, I did.

508
00:28:23,040 --> 00:28:25,120
HR told me about it last week in the team meeting.

509
00:28:26,510 --> 00:28:27,430
Showing they're aware.

510
00:28:27,460 --> 00:28:29,320
What if they've only kind of seen it?

511
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:30,960
Ah, good point.

512
00:28:31,900 --> 00:28:38,510
If you've only partially engaged with it, you might say, yeah, I skimmed through the email, which means you read it quickly just to get the general idea.

513
00:28:38,570 --> 00:28:41,900
Or maybe I glanced at it, meaning you looked very quickly.

514
00:28:42,040 --> 00:28:43,800
Skimmed or glanced.

515
00:28:43,820 --> 00:28:44,500
Useful words.

516
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:45,320
Very useful.

517
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,920
Many of us are busy, so we only have time to skim things initially.

518
00:28:49,670 --> 00:28:50,870
You might elaborate slightly.

519
00:28:51,150 --> 00:28:55,490
Yeah, I read the email pretty quickly, but I need to read more about the actual paperwork requirements later.

520
00:28:55,470 --> 00:28:57,520
Ah, notice the difference there.

521
00:28:58,100 --> 00:29:03,530
You read the email, the specific thing, but read about the paperwork, the topic or details.

522
00:29:04,170 --> 00:29:05,570
Exactly at a sanction again.

523
00:29:05,710 --> 00:29:09,160
And if you're completely unaware, you'd simply say something like, no, I haven't yet.

524
00:29:09,240 --> 00:29:09,820
What happened?

525
00:29:09,900 --> 00:29:11,540
Or no, I didn't.

526
00:29:11,640 --> 00:29:12,300
Is it important?

527
00:29:12,440 --> 00:29:14,220
Or even just no, what's happening?

528
00:29:15,470 --> 00:29:17,550
which clearly invites them to share the news with you.

529
00:29:17,710 --> 00:29:19,120
Opens the door for them to explain.

530
00:29:19,300 --> 00:29:19,560
Yes.

531
00:29:20,040 --> 00:29:26,680
And finally, if you're comfortable with your colleagues and the context allows for it, maybe it's something genuinely frustrating.

532
00:29:27,230 --> 00:29:30,070
You might even have a strong reaction like, yeah, I saw that email.

533
00:29:30,130 --> 00:29:30,510
I hate it.

534
00:29:30,530 --> 00:29:32,390
It's going to add so much time to our process.

535
00:29:32,890 --> 00:29:34,980
But use that kind of response judiciously.

536
00:29:35,100 --> 00:29:36,720
Depends on the workplace culture.

537
00:29:36,850 --> 00:29:37,550
Very much so.

538
00:29:38,250 --> 00:29:41,090
But overall, this type of question, did you hear, read, see?

539
00:29:41,730 --> 00:29:48,620
It's probably one of the best tools for casual office discussions and just keeping abreast of internal news and developments.

540
00:29:48,900 --> 00:29:53,490
It really seems like a vital tool for staying informed and connected within your team or company.

541
00:29:53,930 --> 00:30:02,440
It allows you to participate in discussions about recent company-wide news, ensuring you're not out of the loop, and lets you share or gather perspectives easily.

542
00:30:02,480 --> 00:30:03,100
Absolutely.

543
00:30:03,480 --> 00:30:10,000
It helps you stay plugged into the pulse of the office, which is essential for effective collaboration and just feeling part of the team.

544
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:11,480
Okay, fantastic starters.

545
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:15,140
But now, starting conversations is only half the battle, isn't it?

546
00:30:15,170 --> 00:30:15,850
Oh, absolutely.

547
00:30:16,350 --> 00:30:23,900
Knowing how to politely and professionally end a conversation without offending your colleague is equally important, maybe even more so sometimes.

548
00:30:24,180 --> 00:30:26,290
Yeah, you don't want to seem rude or abrupt.

549
00:30:26,510 --> 00:30:26,990
Exactly.

550
00:30:27,030 --> 00:30:36,900
The goal here is to signal clearly that you need to get back to your work or move on to your next commitment, while also implying, usually, that you're open to chatting again soon.

551
00:30:37,520 --> 00:30:38,860
It preserves the relationship.

552
00:30:38,880 --> 00:30:45,090
This is crucial for time management, obviously, but also for just maintaining professionalism and positive relationships.

553
00:30:45,150 --> 00:30:45,970
Definitely.

554
00:30:45,950 --> 00:30:49,940
It prevents those awkward lingering moments where neither person knows how to break away.

555
00:30:49,960 --> 00:30:54,950
And it ensures your colleagues feel respected, even as you prioritize your tasks.

556
00:30:55,570 --> 00:30:58,140
You don't want to leave someone feeling suddenly cut off or ignored.

557
00:30:58,540 --> 00:31:00,120
No, that feels terrible.

558
00:31:00,200 --> 00:31:04,990
Okay, so let's look at some practical examples of conversation enders.

559
00:31:05,140 --> 00:31:07,510
Maybe we can start with some typically British phrases.

560
00:31:08,350 --> 00:31:11,390
I know Mark, one of our favorite contributors, often uses these.

561
00:31:11,530 --> 00:31:11,830
Right.

562
00:31:11,850 --> 00:31:12,110
Okay.

563
00:31:12,130 --> 00:31:13,040
Some British examples.

564
00:31:13,500 --> 00:31:20,790
My first one starts with a very, very common British word that many of us tend to say just before we frankly stand up or make a move to leave.

565
00:31:20,910 --> 00:31:21,130
Yeah.

566
00:31:21,350 --> 00:31:22,410
The word right.

567
00:31:22,670 --> 00:31:22,800
Right.

568
00:31:22,780 --> 00:31:23,640
Just right.

569
00:31:23,720 --> 00:31:24,800
Just right.

570
00:31:25,460 --> 00:31:35,330
Sometimes accompanied by a subtle physical cue, perhaps like slapping your thighs gently or giving a little pat on your knee, or even just subtly checking your watch.

571
00:31:35,730 --> 00:31:36,940
It's a transition signal.

572
00:31:37,270 --> 00:31:39,750
Okay, so how would the full phrase go?

573
00:31:40,060 --> 00:31:42,380
So you might say, Ryan, well, I should probably get back to my desk now.

574
00:31:42,400 --> 00:31:43,200
I'll chat to you in a bit.

575
00:31:43,620 --> 00:31:46,910
That write is the initial signal, like a little pre-departure announcement.

576
00:31:46,930 --> 00:31:47,910
I'll chat to you in a bit.

577
00:31:47,950 --> 00:31:49,170
That sounds friendly.

578
00:31:49,510 --> 00:31:49,810
It does.

579
00:31:49,850 --> 00:31:50,630
Another common one.

580
00:31:50,930 --> 00:31:53,280
Okay, I've really got to get on with that email I was working on.

581
00:31:53,620 --> 00:31:54,160
Catch up later.

582
00:31:55,940 --> 00:31:58,590
Get on with, meaning continue or start.

583
00:31:58,670 --> 00:32:00,730
Meaning continue or make progress on.

584
00:32:00,970 --> 00:32:01,210
Yeah.

585
00:32:01,910 --> 00:32:05,600
And catch up later is another friendly way to signal future connection.

586
00:32:06,300 --> 00:32:11,120
Or for a quicker exit, maybe if you unexpectedly bump into someone when you're rushing.

587
00:32:11,660 --> 00:32:12,030
Oh, hi.

588
00:32:12,070 --> 00:32:14,670
Sorry, I've actually got a meeting in a few minutes, but let's pick this up later.

589
00:32:14,750 --> 00:32:17,190
Pick this up later, meaning continue the conversation.

590
00:32:17,470 --> 00:32:17,910
Exactly.

591
00:32:18,310 --> 00:32:21,220
Continue this specific conversation at a future time.

592
00:32:21,300 --> 00:32:29,230
What's brilliant about all of those examples, I think, is that they include a phrase that explicitly shows you intend to talk again later.

593
00:32:29,370 --> 00:32:31,230
Yes, that's a key element for politeness.

594
00:32:31,270 --> 00:32:32,070
I'll chat to you in a bit.

595
00:32:32,170 --> 00:32:32,730
Catch up later.

596
00:32:32,780 --> 00:32:33,540
Let's pick this up later.

597
00:32:33,680 --> 00:32:34,900
These aren't final goodbyes.

598
00:32:34,940 --> 00:32:40,390
They convey a desire to reconnect, which sounds keen and friendly, even though you're leaving.

599
00:32:40,530 --> 00:32:41,450
Precisely.

600
00:32:41,430 --> 00:32:47,600
They're perfect for ensuring your colleague feels valued and the social connection is maintained even as you make your exit.

601
00:32:48,160 --> 00:32:53,290
You're basically signaling, I respect our conversation, I enjoyed it, but duty calls right now.

602
00:32:53,810 --> 00:32:56,030
Okay, great British examples.

603
00:32:56,450 --> 00:32:58,730
Now, what about some common American examples?

604
00:32:59,240 --> 00:33:03,300
Kat, another one of our insightful colleagues, often uses these effectively.

605
00:33:03,400 --> 00:33:06,530
All right, moving across the pond, you might hear something like, oh, look at the time.

606
00:33:06,570 --> 00:33:11,290
I've actually got to hop into a meeting, jump into a meeting right now, but let's touch base tomorrow.

607
00:33:11,470 --> 00:33:13,500
Hop into or jump into a meeting.

608
00:33:13,560 --> 00:33:14,760
That sounds very active.

609
00:33:15,100 --> 00:33:15,880
It does, doesn't it?

610
00:33:16,440 --> 00:33:22,310
It means you need to start it imminently, like you're literally about to step into the virtual or physical meeting space.

611
00:33:23,140 --> 00:33:24,280
It conveys urgency.

612
00:33:24,380 --> 00:33:25,560
And touch base.

613
00:33:25,580 --> 00:33:32,790
Touch base is a very common American business idiom meaning to contact each other later, often just for a brief update or check in.

614
00:33:33,290 --> 00:33:39,100
It's a very standard way to suggest a follow up without committing to an immediate lengthy discussion right then.

615
00:33:39,220 --> 00:33:40,360
OK, useful phrase.

616
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:41,080
What else?

617
00:33:41,100 --> 00:33:45,550
Another one might be, OK, well, I've got to tie things up here before my next call.

618
00:33:45,850 --> 00:33:47,530
Let's keep in touch about those results, though.

619
00:33:47,610 --> 00:33:48,710
Tie things up.

620
00:33:48,690 --> 00:34:00,060
If you tie things up, or tie up loose ends, it means you need to finish something or bring it to a conclusion, often a specific task, or maybe a series of small outstanding items.

621
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:03,110
It implies you're in the process of wrapping something up.

622
00:34:03,140 --> 00:34:05,440
And keep in touch is pretty straightforward.

623
00:34:05,480 --> 00:34:06,000
Yes.

624
00:34:06,920 --> 00:34:08,790
Simply means to stay in contact.

625
00:34:09,610 --> 00:34:16,200
This one feels perhaps a bit more direct, especially if you genuinely are pressed for time before another commitment.

626
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:17,530
And sometimes simple is good.

627
00:34:17,850 --> 00:34:19,830
Is there a really simple direct one?

628
00:34:19,910 --> 00:34:20,430
Absolutely.

629
00:34:20,530 --> 00:34:26,680
A very simple and direct one, which you can easily extend for a bit more clarity if needed, is just, well, I should probably get back.

630
00:34:26,720 --> 00:34:27,220
See you later.

631
00:34:27,380 --> 00:34:28,680
Just get back.

632
00:34:28,900 --> 00:34:29,440
Just get back.

633
00:34:29,480 --> 00:34:35,430
You can extend that slightly if you want, like get back to work or get back to my office or get back to the meeting I was in.

634
00:34:35,470 --> 00:34:44,500
It implies you need to return to where you were or what you were doing before the conversation, suggesting a prior commitment you need to resume.

635
00:34:44,980 --> 00:34:46,240
Simple, effective.

636
00:34:46,290 --> 00:34:52,500
And now here's one of my ultimate favorite office cliches, but it's used so often for a very good reason.

637
00:34:53,320 --> 00:34:54,800
Let's circle back to this at a later time.

638
00:34:55,150 --> 00:34:56,070
Ah, circle back.

639
00:34:56,250 --> 00:34:57,050
The classic.

640
00:34:57,070 --> 00:34:58,210
The absolute classic.

641
00:34:58,390 --> 00:35:03,220
If you circle back to something, it literally means you intend to return to that topic and discuss it again later.

642
00:35:03,520 --> 00:35:03,940
However.

643
00:35:03,980 --> 00:35:04,930
There's a however.

644
00:35:04,950 --> 00:35:06,810
I fenced a however.

645
00:35:06,910 --> 00:35:08,850
There is often a however.

646
00:35:09,630 --> 00:35:11,720
This is where the deeper cultural insight comes in.

647
00:35:12,420 --> 00:35:13,460
Let's circle back to this.

648
00:35:14,300 --> 00:35:15,680
Frequently has a dual meaning.

649
00:35:16,490 --> 00:35:25,680
Many, many times, if someone says, let's circle back to that, particularly if they say it somewhat dismissively, it can be a very polite way to say, I'm not actually going to talk about that right now.

650
00:35:25,700 --> 00:35:27,540
And maybe I don't intend to talk about it ever again.

651
00:35:27,700 --> 00:35:28,110
Wow.

652
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:29,830
So it can be a polite brush off.

653
00:35:29,960 --> 00:35:31,460
It absolutely can be.

654
00:35:31,820 --> 00:35:36,250
The key to spotting the difference usually is context and nonverbal cues.

655
00:35:36,970 --> 00:35:38,010
Look at their body language.

656
00:35:38,030 --> 00:35:39,110
Are they making eye contact?

657
00:35:39,130 --> 00:35:41,960
Do they sound genuinely interested in rescheduling the discussion?

658
00:35:42,440 --> 00:35:45,320
Also, consider the urgency and importance of the original topic.

659
00:35:45,880 --> 00:35:52,130
And maybe the most crucial indicator, if they initiate the follow-up later, then it was probably a genuine deferral.

660
00:35:52,150 --> 00:35:53,010
And if they don't?

661
00:35:53,090 --> 00:36:00,900
If they don't bring it up again and the topic never resurfaces from their end, you might have been politely dismissed or put off indefinitely.

662
00:36:01,540 --> 00:36:07,700
And in most cases, it's generally best to take that cue and not aggressively pursue it unless it's critically important.

663
00:36:07,860 --> 00:36:08,600
Fascinating.

664
00:36:09,060 --> 00:36:13,990
That dual meaning of circle back is definitely something to be aware of.

665
00:36:14,050 --> 00:36:15,990
It's such a common phrase for a reason.

666
00:36:16,330 --> 00:36:17,530
It serves multiple purposes.

667
00:36:18,010 --> 00:36:28,240
And finally, for a really quick exit, maybe especially if you're caught on the phone or you genuinely need to leave immediately, you can use something like, sorry, I've actually got to run now, but I'll check in later.

668
00:36:28,360 --> 00:36:28,880
Got to run.

669
00:36:29,040 --> 00:36:30,820
Got to run just means you've got to leave quickly.

670
00:36:30,840 --> 00:36:31,980
You need to depart urgently.

671
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:36,570
It's a concise, signals urgency, and the check in later softens it.

672
00:36:36,630 --> 00:36:36,970
Okay.

673
00:36:37,350 --> 00:36:45,150
Mastering these endings seems truly crucial, not just for politeness, but for effective time management and maintaining that professional image.

674
00:36:45,410 --> 00:36:45,910
Absolutely.

675
00:36:46,210 --> 00:36:53,460
It prevents that awkward lingering, ensures colleagues feel respected even as you prioritize your work, and just makes daily interactions smoother.

676
00:36:53,780 --> 00:37:01,610
It's all about gracefully transitioning from social interaction back to productivity, which is a core skill in any business English environment, really.

677
00:37:01,680 --> 00:37:07,070
So to recap, we've covered a huge range of different ways to confidently start those English conversations at work.

678
00:37:07,450 --> 00:37:10,700
Everything from just checking in on someone's day, like, how's it been?

679
00:37:10,740 --> 00:37:12,040
Or how's it going?

680
00:37:12,360 --> 00:37:16,990
To seeking quite detailed opinions on new projects or company changes.

681
00:37:17,270 --> 00:37:20,690
Using how do you like or what are your thoughts on.

682
00:37:20,830 --> 00:37:24,960
Or even just getting up to speed on the latest company news with did you hear about.

683
00:37:25,580 --> 00:37:33,880
And just as importantly, we've given you a whole toolkit of phrases to politely and professionally bring those conversations to a close when you need to.

684
00:37:34,300 --> 00:37:34,560
Yeah.

685
00:37:34,580 --> 00:37:40,330
And understanding and practicing these phrases isn't just about, you know, memorizing English words.

686
00:37:40,370 --> 00:37:44,180
It's really about learning to navigate workplace culture effectively.

687
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:45,160
Building that rapport.

688
00:37:45,300 --> 00:37:45,980
Building rapport.

689
00:37:46,200 --> 00:37:46,820
Absolutely.

690
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:49,790
And ensuring clear, unambiguous communication.

691
00:37:50,560 --> 00:38:00,230
Each of these structures we've discussed helps you manage the flow of information, express yourself appropriately for the situation, and even subtly influence how you're perceived professionally.

692
00:38:00,250 --> 00:38:01,280
It gives you more confidence.

693
00:38:01,400 --> 00:38:07,620
It gives you much greater confidence, enabling you to connect, collaborate, and contribute effectively within your professional environment.

694
00:38:07,980 --> 00:38:12,050
Okay, so now that you, our listeners, have these tools, how about you actually try them out?

695
00:38:12,730 --> 00:38:16,530
For this deep dive, we really want you to engage actively with this.

696
00:38:16,840 --> 00:38:17,940
Put it into practice.

697
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:18,920
Exactly.

698
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:20,650
Think about your own workday.

699
00:38:20,710 --> 00:38:24,460
How would you answer right now if someone asked you, how's your day going?

700
00:38:25,050 --> 00:38:27,980
Or maybe, how do you like your current project so far?

701
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:34,680
Spend a moment actually forming those replies in your mind using the structures and vocabulary we've discussed.

702
00:38:34,780 --> 00:38:35,740
Maybe you can say them out loud.

703
00:38:35,860 --> 00:38:36,180
Yeah.

704
00:38:36,620 --> 00:38:38,480
Imagine a colleague is approaching you right now.

705
00:38:39,020 --> 00:38:41,330
How would you greet them using one of these starters?

706
00:38:41,990 --> 00:38:48,800
And then if you needed to excuse yourself after a brief chat, how would you gracefully end the conversation using one of the enders?

707
00:38:48,850 --> 00:38:51,050
And this raises an important question for you to consider.

708
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:58,660
What conversations will you perhaps start or end differently tomorrow now that you're armed with these new insights and phrases?

709
00:38:58,750 --> 00:39:00,380
Hmm, a practical challenge.

710
00:39:00,720 --> 00:39:05,920
It is, because the true value of this kind of knowledge really lies in its application.

711
00:39:06,500 --> 00:39:07,920
So go forth and connect.

712
00:39:08,530 --> 00:39:12,610
And maybe pay attention to how your English-speaking colleagues use these phrases, too.

713
00:39:12,930 --> 00:39:14,970
It's excellent ear training to hear them in context.

714
00:39:15,030 --> 00:39:15,950
That's a great final tip.

715
00:39:16,430 --> 00:39:17,880
Okay, that's all for this deep dive.

716
00:39:18,020 --> 00:39:19,100
Thank you so much for joining us.

717
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:19,860
Thanks, everyone.

718
00:39:20,100 --> 00:39:22,400
And we'll be back soon with more fascinating insights.

719
00:39:22,980 --> 00:39:25,970
It's a true classic, really versatile, and widely used.

720
00:39:26,350 --> 00:39:27,650
How's your day been?

721
00:39:27,880 --> 00:39:31,050
Now, the core structure here is how has something been?

722
00:39:31,110 --> 00:39:32,170
Sounds simple, right?

723
00:39:32,570 --> 00:39:34,810
But there's a specific reason for that has been.

724
00:39:34,850 --> 00:39:39,500
And that reason, which, you know, often trips up learners, it's all about the present perfect tense.

725
00:39:39,720 --> 00:39:39,960
Yeah.

726
00:39:40,080 --> 00:39:46,990
We use has been specifically because when you're asking this at work, your workday is, well, almost certainly still ongoing.

727
00:39:47,050 --> 00:39:47,380
Yeah.

728
00:39:47,400 --> 00:39:51,960
It's before 5.00 p.m. or maybe 5.30 p.m., so the day hasn't actually concluded yet.

729
00:39:51,940 --> 00:39:56,350
You're inquiring about a continuing state about everything that has happened up until this very moment.

730
00:39:56,650 --> 00:39:59,700
You're kind of inviting an update on their whole experience of the day so far.

731
00:40:00,320 --> 00:40:04,790
OK, so it's that sense of ongoing time that makes the present perfect fit so well here.

732
00:40:04,830 --> 00:40:07,330
But what's the sort of deeper purpose of asking this?

733
00:40:07,430 --> 00:40:08,290
Is it just being polite?

734
00:40:08,510 --> 00:40:13,040
Well, politeness is part of it, but its purpose is actually quite powerful.

735
00:40:13,020 --> 00:40:25,090
It's a friendly way to inquire about someone's general experience, their mood, maybe their workload, but also crucially about their specific work-related progress without being too direct or nosy.

736
00:40:25,410 --> 00:40:32,380
You're signaling genuine interest, opening a casual line for updates and showing you care about their well-being and their work.

737
00:40:32,400 --> 00:40:35,220
It's really a low-pressure way to initiate connection.

738
00:40:35,320 --> 00:40:35,940
That makes sense.

739
00:40:36,460 --> 00:40:38,540
And we've got some great practical examples for you.

740
00:40:38,640 --> 00:40:41,010
Beyond the standard, how's your day been?

741
00:40:41,030 --> 00:40:42,710
Oh, you could expand it like...

742
00:40:42,690 --> 00:40:43,510
How's your week been?

743
00:40:43,750 --> 00:40:47,540
Yeah, that's super useful, especially midweek, like on a Wednesday when the week isn't quite over.

744
00:40:47,940 --> 00:40:50,600
Or to be more precise, maybe earlier in the day.

745
00:40:50,660 --> 00:40:52,570
How's your morning been so far?

746
00:40:53,230 --> 00:40:56,350
That little phrase so far really means until now, doesn't it?

747
00:40:56,830 --> 00:41:01,200
It reinforces that present perfect idea and invites a summary of just their morning.

748
00:41:01,180 --> 00:41:01,780
Exactly.

749
00:41:01,880 --> 00:41:06,290
And to make it even more work specific, you can directly ask about projects or initiatives.

750
00:41:06,330 --> 00:41:07,530
How's your new project been?

751
00:41:07,790 --> 00:41:12,820
You're clearly asking for updates on that specific work thing that is currently ongoing.

752
00:41:13,320 --> 00:41:19,200
It's a perfect, non-intrusive way to show engagement and maybe keep tabs on shared goals.

753
00:41:19,490 --> 00:41:20,570
Okay, so what about replies?

754
00:41:21,490 --> 00:41:25,010
When it comes to replies, there are some interesting nuances, I think.

755
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:27,140
If someone asks you, how's your day been?

756
00:41:27,180 --> 00:41:32,010
I know a very common, almost understated British answer is often, yeah, not too bad.

757
00:41:32,890 --> 00:41:33,970
Yes, very British.

758
00:41:34,190 --> 00:41:38,970
It's casual, polite, and kind of avoids oversharing, which is very much a British trait sometimes.

759
00:41:39,290 --> 00:41:46,500
But you can, of course, use the full present perfect in your reply, mirroring the question, it's been busy, or maybe it's been fine.

760
00:41:47,140 --> 00:41:55,070
However, and here's a fascinating thing about native speakers, many will simply drop the it's been and just say one word, busy.

761
00:41:55,050 --> 00:41:56,310
Or fine.

762
00:41:56,430 --> 00:41:57,730
Yeah, I hear that all the time.

763
00:41:57,870 --> 00:41:58,510
How's your day been?

764
00:41:58,990 --> 00:41:59,970
Busy.

765
00:41:59,990 --> 00:42:00,860
It's efficient, isn't it?

766
00:42:01,300 --> 00:42:01,600
It is.

767
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:09,250
It's extremely common in casual workplace chats, and it's perfectly acceptable even if it technically breaks a grammar rule.

768
00:42:09,270 --> 00:42:11,910
It sounds incredibly natural and, well, efficient.

769
00:42:12,560 --> 00:42:15,840
Another common positive response is just, it's been good.

770
00:42:16,620 --> 00:42:20,970
And here's a really useful broader phrase, things have been good.

771
00:42:21,290 --> 00:42:21,570
Yes.

772
00:42:21,670 --> 00:42:31,530
When you say things have been good, that things refers broadly to life events, updates, news, or just the general situation beyond the immediate work tasks.

773
00:42:31,550 --> 00:42:33,330
It's a broader positive update.

774
00:42:33,830 --> 00:42:33,930
Okay.

775
00:42:33,950 --> 00:42:38,760
And if you're talking about a specific project, a quick contraction is very common.

776
00:42:38,960 --> 00:42:43,010
The project's been good, it's fast, it's natural, and it gets the point across quickly.

777
00:42:43,040 --> 00:42:56,740
So the power of this structure for you, the listener, is that it allows you to show genuine interest in your colleagues, smoothly open a line for updates without being too pushy, and really initiate a warm, ongoing connection in your workplace English conversations.

778
00:42:57,160 --> 00:42:59,620
It's kind of the bedrock of effective business small talk.

779
00:42:59,860 --> 00:43:01,880
All right, moving on to our second simple starter.

780
00:43:02,840 --> 00:43:08,690
This one is very similar in meaning to our first, but uses a subtly different grammatical structure.

781
00:43:09,230 --> 00:43:10,050
How's your day going?

782
00:43:10,650 --> 00:43:13,320
The core structure is how is something going?

783
00:43:13,620 --> 00:43:13,820
Right.

784
00:43:13,900 --> 00:43:20,890
This uses the present continuous, again, because the activity, your day, your work is continuing and not finished yet.

785
00:43:21,530 --> 00:43:25,490
While it's grammatically distinct from how's your day been, it carries a very similar meaning.

786
00:43:26,190 --> 00:43:33,880
You're inquiring about current progress or very recent activities, usually within the last few hours or even the current moment.

787
00:43:34,260 --> 00:43:36,390
It's about the flow of events right now.

788
00:43:36,430 --> 00:43:38,130
It feels very immediate, very present.

789
00:43:38,640 --> 00:43:45,280
It's just a nice, easy way to check in with a colleague about their current day at work or how things have been progressing recently.

790
00:43:45,450 --> 00:43:45,910
Exactly.

791
00:43:46,050 --> 00:43:47,910
And beyond the general, how's your day going?

792
00:43:47,930 --> 00:43:49,610
Oh, you can make it more specific.

793
00:43:49,730 --> 00:43:50,690
How's your work going?

794
00:43:51,290 --> 00:43:57,720
Or if someone has recently, say, transitioned into a new position or has changed responsibilities, you can ask, how's your new role going?

795
00:43:57,840 --> 00:43:58,000
Right.

796
00:43:58,040 --> 00:44:04,530
That could be moving from, say, an assistant to a team lead or maybe a new job title or simply new duties.

797
00:44:04,510 --> 00:44:05,090
Precisely.

798
00:44:05,130 --> 00:44:12,840
And if they're involved in professional development or maybe some kind of training, a perfect question is, how is your training going?

799
00:44:13,200 --> 00:44:15,120
Or just like before, how's your new project going?

800
00:44:15,220 --> 00:44:19,570
These are all about asking about ongoing activities that are currently unfolding.

801
00:44:19,810 --> 00:44:21,050
Okay, now for the replies.

802
00:44:21,350 --> 00:44:22,990
This is where it gets interesting, I think.

803
00:44:23,460 --> 00:44:28,760
Since the question starts with how, we typically use an adverb to describe the manner, right?

804
00:44:29,220 --> 00:44:31,570
So, yeah, it's going well.

805
00:44:31,550 --> 00:44:35,090
It's a very standard and, you know, grammatically correct response.

806
00:44:35,430 --> 00:44:35,750
That's right.

807
00:44:35,790 --> 00:44:38,140
You could also say, yeah, it's going quite smoothly.

808
00:44:38,200 --> 00:44:40,240
Or, yeah, it's going quickly, actually.

809
00:44:40,280 --> 00:44:43,170
These all use adverbs that describe how something is going.

810
00:44:43,210 --> 00:44:45,150
And I hear this one all the time.

811
00:44:45,170 --> 00:44:48,480
And I know it might go against the traditional grammar books, but it's so common.

812
00:44:48,860 --> 00:44:49,960
How is your new role going?

813
00:44:50,400 --> 00:44:51,220
Oh, it's going good.

814
00:44:51,200 --> 00:44:55,950
Yes, that's a fascinating nuance and it's almost a hallmark of casual American English.

815
00:44:56,410 --> 00:45:05,950
While technically, yes, good is an adjective and well is the adverb, it's going good, is a very, very common casual and friendly American English response.

816
00:45:06,610 --> 00:45:12,640
It's generally not considered rude or wrong in informal workplace settings, especially if you're feeling positive.

817
00:45:12,620 --> 00:45:23,580
In fact, saying good can sometimes drop the formality just a little signaling you're comfortable, maybe even enthusiastic, whereas well remains the traditionally correct choice.

818
00:45:23,840 --> 00:45:26,020
It's a subtle way to build rapport, actually.

819
00:45:26,220 --> 00:45:34,010
So it's incredibly useful to know the correct grammar, but also what's just commonly accepted and maybe even preferred in casual chat.

820
00:45:34,730 --> 00:45:39,840
Another very common and quite neutral reply is it's going OK.

821
00:45:39,990 --> 00:45:47,780
Ah, OK. Now, this response, while it seems neutral on the surface, is often a very polite way to say things aren't actually great without complaining.

822
00:45:48,220 --> 00:45:49,820
Think of it maybe as a five out of 10.

823
00:45:49,890 --> 00:45:53,590
It kind of implies mediocre at best or I'm managing, but it's nothing special.

824
00:45:53,750 --> 00:45:54,190
Yeah.

825
00:45:54,210 --> 00:46:00,020
And this can be quite a strategic response because generally you want to avoid getting a reputation for complaining or moaning at work.

826
00:46:00,420 --> 00:46:04,080
Nobody really wants to be that person who always brings the office mood down.

827
00:46:04,280 --> 00:46:04,660
Right.

828
00:46:04,760 --> 00:46:05,540
That makes a lot of sense.

829
00:46:05,560 --> 00:46:08,210
It's almost like a little professional shield to manage your reputation.

830
00:46:08,510 --> 00:46:09,990
What about other useful replies?

831
00:46:10,030 --> 00:46:11,550
Like, it's coming along.

832
00:46:11,750 --> 00:46:12,010
Yeah.

833
00:46:12,340 --> 00:46:13,900
It's coming along means we're working on it.

834
00:46:14,400 --> 00:46:18,160
Indicating steady progress, but maybe not necessarily fast progress.

835
00:46:18,590 --> 00:46:22,290
It implies, you know, progress is being made, maybe slowly, but it is happening.

836
00:46:22,330 --> 00:46:22,790
Okay.

837
00:46:23,170 --> 00:46:26,420
And a more robust answer if things are proving difficult.

838
00:46:26,400 --> 00:46:29,060
It's tough, but we're getting through it.

839
00:46:29,100 --> 00:46:35,190
This indicates a challenge, but also perseverance, that kind of step-by-step approach that signals resilience.

840
00:46:35,830 --> 00:46:45,730
This really highlights how these phrases allow you, the listener, to gauge a colleague's current state and can open the door for you to offer potential support or simply show you care.

841
00:46:46,350 --> 00:46:50,710
It fosters better workplace relationships and ultimately a more positive business environment.

842
00:46:50,810 --> 00:46:51,590
Definitely.

843
00:46:51,730 --> 00:46:53,770
Okay, let's move to our third how question.

844
00:46:54,330 --> 00:47:00,760
It's one slightly longer, but incredibly flexible, especially when you're looking for broader updates about people or maybe groups.

845
00:47:01,460 --> 00:47:02,660
How are things going with your team?

846
00:47:03,280 --> 00:47:06,350
The core structure is how are things going with person, group, object?

847
00:47:06,810 --> 00:47:15,380
This, again, uses the present continuous because the situation with the team or the client or the boss is, well, ongoing and dynamic.

848
00:47:15,400 --> 00:47:18,380
The real beauty of this question is its remarkable versatility.

849
00:47:18,800 --> 00:47:20,800
You're asking about people they work with.

850
00:47:21,410 --> 00:47:24,490
directly their boss, maybe specific groups or clients.

851
00:47:24,660 --> 00:47:26,420
You're subtly seeking updates.

852
00:47:26,820 --> 00:47:30,870
You want to know if things are going well, just okay, or maybe there are some challenges.

853
00:47:31,730 --> 00:47:41,650
And importantly, you're signaling that you're willing to potentially help or offer advice if there are problems, making it a polite, appropriate, and friendly way to check in.

854
00:47:41,630 --> 00:47:42,070
That's right.

855
00:47:42,710 --> 00:47:47,500
It could be, how are things going with your client, especially if they've recently taken on a new key account?

856
00:47:48,020 --> 00:47:49,860
Or how are things going with your new boss?

857
00:47:50,060 --> 00:47:52,670
Or maybe new superior or new supervisor?

858
00:47:52,850 --> 00:47:53,210
Yeah.

859
00:47:53,230 --> 00:47:55,470
You can even ask, how are things going with the new guy?

860
00:47:55,780 --> 00:48:00,220
Referring to a recent hire in a friendly, casual way, perhaps just to see if they're settling in okay.

861
00:48:00,640 --> 00:48:03,550
It extends beyond just individuals or small groups, right?

862
00:48:03,910 --> 00:48:05,810
Like how are things going with your new department?

863
00:48:06,470 --> 00:48:08,270
Or even more generally, how are things going?

864
00:48:08,510 --> 00:48:09,150
Exactly.

865
00:48:09,700 --> 00:48:17,440
That last one can be asked simply about life outside work, but it's easily adapted to work topics, even hobbies if you're trying to build rapport.

866
00:48:18,050 --> 00:48:21,070
How are things going with that art project you mentioned?

867
00:48:21,730 --> 00:48:29,600
It's a great small talk question that you can easily tailor to the situation, making it perfect for both professional and casual business conversations.

868
00:48:29,640 --> 00:48:30,280
Very flexible.

869
00:48:30,260 --> 00:48:31,700
It is very, very flexible.

870
00:48:31,940 --> 00:48:34,610
And here's a crucial point, especially for international listeners.

871
00:48:35,270 --> 00:48:41,260
Unlike some other languages where you might directly translate, you generally wouldn't say, how's your boss going in English?

872
00:48:41,320 --> 00:48:43,200
It just sounds odd.

873
00:48:43,680 --> 00:48:43,800
Right.

874
00:48:43,820 --> 00:48:53,080
This structure, how are things going with person group, is the natural and appropriate way to ask about people and their situations in an English speaking workplace.

875
00:48:53,410 --> 00:48:57,000
OK, so for replies, if someone asks you, how are things going with your new team?

876
00:48:57,940 --> 00:49:00,680
You could say, yeah, it's going well or it's going smoothly.

877
00:49:01,300 --> 00:49:04,770
Again, using those adverbs to describe how the situation is progressing.

878
00:49:05,370 --> 00:49:15,700
And as we discussed with it's going good, the casual, yeah, it's going good or it's going great are also perfectly acceptable, particularly in American English, if you want a more relaxed tone.

879
00:49:16,390 --> 00:49:22,570
Since the work or the relationship is ongoing, you can also use the present perfect to describe a state leading up to now.

880
00:49:24,060 --> 00:49:27,250
So how are things going with your new boss?

881
00:49:27,820 --> 00:49:29,500
It's been tricky so far.

882
00:49:29,540 --> 00:49:31,360
We're still kind of figuring out the workflow.

883
00:49:31,600 --> 00:49:31,880
Right.

884
00:49:32,450 --> 00:49:35,950
Or on the more positive side, it's been pretty good so far.

885
00:49:36,030 --> 00:49:39,130
We've been getting to know each other and, you know, establishing our roles.

886
00:49:39,490 --> 00:49:44,880
Other continuous updates could be things like, we're making a lot of progress or we're getting a lot done.

887
00:49:45,240 --> 00:49:49,080
These are both positive and show continuous work and productivity.

888
00:49:49,100 --> 00:49:52,370
And for general updates, things are going really well or everything's going well.

889
00:49:52,410 --> 00:49:55,230
But then there's that nuanced, they're going okay.

890
00:49:55,250 --> 00:49:57,150
But yes, the strategic, okay.

891
00:49:57,320 --> 00:49:57,940
Exactly.

892
00:49:57,980 --> 00:50:04,670
Again, this is often the minimum OK, a polite way to convey that things aren't fantastic, but you're managing.

893
00:50:05,010 --> 00:50:07,250
It's about managing your professional reputation.

894
00:50:07,630 --> 00:50:14,880
It stops you developing that reputation for complaining or moaning, which, as we said, can really affect the morale and productivity of an office.

895
00:50:15,760 --> 00:50:21,250
So often, OK is the safest and most appropriate level of negativity to share at work.

896
00:50:21,310 --> 00:50:24,020
This really raises an important point for you, the listener.

897
00:50:24,660 --> 00:50:32,090
How do these questions allow you to discreetly offer assistance, build rapport, and truly understand the dynamics a colleague might be facing?

898
00:50:32,490 --> 00:50:36,020
It strengthens your professional network and makes you a more valuable team member.

899
00:50:36,620 --> 00:50:39,050
It's about being observant and supportive, isn't it?

900
00:50:39,230 --> 00:50:39,670
Absolutely.

901
00:50:39,710 --> 00:50:41,750
It's active listening built into the questions.

902
00:50:41,730 --> 00:50:43,260
Okay, now let's shift gears slightly.

903
00:50:43,480 --> 00:50:44,780
We've talked about general updates.

904
00:50:45,100 --> 00:50:50,190
Now let's move to asking for opinions and feedback, which is so crucial for collaborative work.

905
00:50:50,750 --> 00:50:53,490
Our next phrase is, how do you like the job so far?

906
00:50:54,070 --> 00:50:54,840
The core structure.

907
00:50:55,320 --> 00:50:56,840
How do you like something so far?

908
00:50:56,990 --> 00:51:01,160
This question is incredibly insightful, mainly because of that initial how.

909
00:51:01,720 --> 00:51:03,300
It's a really key distinction.

910
00:51:03,820 --> 00:51:09,670
If you simply ask, do you like the job so far, the answer would likely be a simple yes or no.

911
00:51:10,310 --> 00:51:15,780
But by starting with how, you are explicitly asking for more than just a binary response.

912
00:51:16,200 --> 00:51:24,530
You're prompting your colleague to provide their opinion, to explain why they like it, or what their experience has been like, not just a simple thumbs up or thumbs down.

913
00:51:24,510 --> 00:51:29,100
It pushes them to share a deeper, more detailed perspective.

914
00:51:29,120 --> 00:51:31,740
And that so far is really important here, too, isn't it?

915
00:51:31,760 --> 00:51:38,870
Meaning up until this moment, this question is used when something new has started at work, maybe a new role, a new initiative.

916
00:51:39,260 --> 00:51:44,120
And you're asking about their experience after, say, a few days or weeks of it being underway.

917
00:51:44,100 --> 00:51:44,780
Exactly.

918
00:51:45,110 --> 00:51:48,960
For a brand new colleague who just started, how do you like the job so far is perfect.

919
00:51:49,380 --> 00:51:54,230
Or if your company introduced, say, a new work from home policy, you can ask, how do you like working from home so far?

920
00:51:54,570 --> 00:51:56,170
You're basically asking, are you enjoying it?

921
00:51:56,210 --> 00:51:57,040
Is it working for you?

922
00:51:57,100 --> 00:51:57,840
Is it a bit weird?

923
00:51:57,920 --> 00:51:59,560
What are your impressions?

924
00:52:00,180 --> 00:52:02,490
And it applies to specific aspects of the job, too.

925
00:52:03,430 --> 00:52:05,550
How do you like the team dynamics so far?

926
00:52:05,670 --> 00:52:07,440
Or how do you like the new role so far?

927
00:52:07,480 --> 00:52:08,720
How do you like the company so far?

928
00:52:08,860 --> 00:52:10,820
Even how do you like the project so far?

929
00:52:11,420 --> 00:52:15,290
You're trying to gauge their experience and overall impression of something ongoing.

930
00:52:15,440 --> 00:52:18,650
And, you know, these conversations aren't just polite filler.

931
00:52:18,710 --> 00:52:20,710
They can actually become quite meaningful.

932
00:52:21,210 --> 00:52:31,490
When you ask this, you're often also asking because you might be able to help or offer some advice or maybe provide some reassurance if they're finding things challenging or a bit overwhelming.

933
00:52:32,110 --> 00:52:35,440
It's a genuine gesture of support within a professional context.

934
00:52:35,420 --> 00:52:36,620
That's a great point.

935
00:52:36,640 --> 00:52:39,190
So if your colleague asks you this, how can you reply?

936
00:52:40,130 --> 00:52:43,050
A good straightforward start is, yeah, it's been really great so far.

937
00:52:43,850 --> 00:52:48,820
That so far definitely influences the grammar, leading you naturally towards the present perfect.

938
00:52:48,980 --> 00:52:50,580
Or I've been enjoying it so far.

939
00:52:51,280 --> 00:52:56,130
Another excellent option, especially if you want to be brief but positive, is everything is going well so far.

940
00:52:56,710 --> 00:52:59,540
That so far is a clear signal to use the present perfect.

941
00:53:00,020 --> 00:53:05,220
And a very common, simple, and neutrally positive phrase you hear a lot is, so far, so good.

942
00:53:05,460 --> 00:53:07,400
I love so far, so good.

943
00:53:07,900 --> 00:53:11,490
It's concise and just implies things are on track.

944
00:53:11,510 --> 00:53:17,500
And if you want to provide a bit more detail, giving a more nuanced opinion, you could say something like, I really like the project.

945
00:53:17,740 --> 00:53:19,910
It's challenging, but it's really interesting.

946
00:53:20,120 --> 00:53:20,720
Absolutely.

947
00:53:20,760 --> 00:53:24,910
You're explaining the why, providing concrete reasons for your opinion.

948
00:53:25,330 --> 00:53:28,860
For instance, you might say, honestly, it's been great so far.

949
00:53:29,000 --> 00:53:32,320
People are really friendly and the training has been very eye opening.

950
00:53:32,360 --> 00:53:39,810
This provides rich detail, showcasing the depth of response this type of question can and often does elicit.

951
00:53:40,350 --> 00:53:44,560
It shows you're engaged and thoughtful, which is always valued in a professional setting.

952
00:53:44,640 --> 00:53:51,070
So this is a really good tactic for you to extract more information from your colleagues if you want a longer, more insightful answer.

953
00:53:51,930 --> 00:53:57,110
It moves beyond simple yes-no responses and helps you genuinely understand their experience.

954
00:53:57,850 --> 00:54:04,040
It's excellent for building connections and getting a deeper read on the office pulse in your daily workplace English-speaking practice.

955
00:54:04,340 --> 00:54:10,880
Okay, our next opinion question is slightly less formal than the one we'll look at next, but still perfectly appropriate for the workplace.

956
00:54:12,200 --> 00:54:14,330
What do you think about Sarah's idea?

957
00:54:14,850 --> 00:54:18,450
The core structure is what do you think about idea suggestion change?

958
00:54:18,950 --> 00:54:19,190
Right.

959
00:54:19,850 --> 00:54:26,720
This question is really designed for discussing ideas, potential decisions or changes after a meeting or maybe a team discussion.

960
00:54:27,180 --> 00:54:30,260
You're trying to get your colleagues professional opinion.

961
00:54:30,510 --> 00:54:31,550
Did they like the idea?

962
00:54:31,570 --> 00:54:32,530
Do they disagree?

963
00:54:32,570 --> 00:54:33,890
Do they have a different perspective?

964
00:54:34,190 --> 00:54:34,590
Yeah.

965
00:54:34,610 --> 00:54:40,120
And it's important to stress this is about work related professional feedback, not office gossip.

966
00:54:40,500 --> 00:54:44,160
It's completely appropriate for private one on one conversations.

967
00:54:44,140 --> 00:54:44,760
Exactly.

968
00:54:45,300 --> 00:54:49,670
It's great for those face to face chats, maybe in the break room or swinging by someone's desk.

969
00:54:50,110 --> 00:54:51,870
Like, what do you think about the boss's suggestion?

970
00:54:52,150 --> 00:54:53,140
It's quite ambitious, right?

971
00:54:53,160 --> 00:54:55,260
Do you think we can actually do that given our resources?

972
00:54:55,520 --> 00:54:56,980
Or what do you think about the new schedule?

973
00:54:57,480 --> 00:55:02,210
Maybe the company is experimenting with starting at eight instead of nine and you want to know their honest thoughts.

974
00:55:02,730 --> 00:55:04,630
It could also be about larger company shifts.

975
00:55:04,670 --> 00:55:06,370
Like, what do you think about the merger?

976
00:55:06,430 --> 00:55:07,640
Ah, that's a good one.

977
00:55:07,610 --> 00:55:17,430
Yeah, it's a much better way to gauge professional feelings about a big company change without asking the potentially too personal, how do you feel about it?

978
00:55:18,470 --> 00:55:22,160
Which might invite emotional responses rather than professional insights.

979
00:55:22,400 --> 00:55:22,900
Good point.

980
00:55:23,460 --> 00:55:28,510
And then there's the more general, what do you think about that email they send out from HR regarding the new policy?

981
00:55:29,250 --> 00:55:32,530
Now, for replies to, what do you think about?

982
00:55:33,470 --> 00:55:36,460
If someone asks, what do you think about the boss's suggestion?

983
00:55:37,550 --> 00:55:39,960
You could say, I think it makes sense.

984
00:55:39,980 --> 00:55:44,730
This basically means I understand the idea and it seems logical or potentially good.

985
00:55:44,790 --> 00:55:47,040
OK. Or a bit more cautiously.

986
00:55:47,080 --> 00:55:49,580
I think it might work, but I'm not sure.

987
00:55:50,100 --> 00:55:52,870
And if you're really not convinced, I'm not totally convinced yet.

988
00:55:52,990 --> 00:55:54,300
I need to see more data.

989
00:55:54,480 --> 00:56:01,770
And for a more general summary, especially when you have pros and cons but haven't fully collected your thoughts yet, you could use the word overall.

990
00:56:01,830 --> 00:56:05,610
Like, I think overall it's a good idea, or I think overall it probably won't work in the long run.

991
00:56:06,390 --> 00:56:11,380
It's a quick, useful summary that allows you to express a balanced view without getting lost in the weeds.

992
00:56:11,880 --> 00:56:15,220
Or you can just be honest and transparent and say, I'm not sure what I think yet.

993
00:56:15,680 --> 00:56:17,500
It has pros and it has cons.

994
00:56:18,090 --> 00:56:21,010
Now, this could be a genuine admission that you're still processing.

995
00:56:20,990 --> 00:56:27,700
Or frankly, a polite way to avoid discussing it if you don't have enough information or maybe just don't want to talk about it right then.

996
00:56:27,720 --> 00:56:27,880
Right.

997
00:56:27,940 --> 00:56:28,960
A polite sidestep.

998
00:56:29,000 --> 00:56:29,540
Exactly.

999
00:56:29,580 --> 00:56:35,630
And if you feel an idea needs improvement, you might say, I think it could be better or I think it could use some work.

1000
00:56:36,270 --> 00:56:40,800
This suggests refinement rather than outright rejection, which is often more constructive.

1001
00:56:41,200 --> 00:56:45,130
And similar to overall, you can use in general.

1002
00:56:45,210 --> 00:56:49,170
Like I think in general, it's not the best idea for our team right now.

1003
00:56:49,680 --> 00:56:54,980
These are good summary words when your thoughts aren't fully formed, but you want to offer a high level opinion.

1004
00:56:55,950 --> 00:57:07,470
So these phrases really empower you to confidently engage in discussions about workplace changes and ideas, offering your perspective or understanding others without over committing or becoming too formal.

1005
00:57:07,660 --> 00:57:12,100
It's that practical small talk for the office that can lead to really productive dialogue.

1006
00:57:12,480 --> 00:57:14,780
Now let's step up the formality just a bit.

1007
00:57:15,390 --> 00:57:18,310
Our next question is, what are your thoughts on the new project?

1008
00:57:18,800 --> 00:57:22,690
The core structure is what are your thoughts on project presentation software?

1009
00:57:22,800 --> 00:57:23,220
OK.

1010
00:57:23,240 --> 00:57:30,700
So when you ask this question or when someone asks you this, you're signaling that you're asking for a more elaborate, professional and often longer answer.

1011
00:57:31,280 --> 00:57:37,800
It implies that you expect details, maybe examples to support opinions and a more considered formal response.

1012
00:57:38,020 --> 00:57:38,140
Right.

1013
00:57:39,070 --> 00:57:47,640
This question is definitely more suitable for a meeting setting, maybe a formal email, or when your boss asks for your professional opinion on a pretty serious matter.

1014
00:57:48,560 --> 00:57:52,000
Be prepared to talk and share quite a bit more detail when you're asked this.

1015
00:57:52,220 --> 00:57:55,540
And here's a really crucial piece of advice for you, the listener.

1016
00:57:56,120 --> 00:58:05,390
Even if you personally hate some new software because, I don't know, the buttons are all weird and it's slow and doesn't work, you do not say that directly in this context.

1017
00:58:05,810 --> 00:58:08,960
Your response needs to be professional and analytical.

1018
00:58:09,530 --> 00:58:12,350
Instead, you could say something like, well, it needs some work.

1019
00:58:12,730 --> 00:58:14,540
There are still some kinks to iron out.

1020
00:58:15,240 --> 00:58:17,580
Kinks meaning small problems or issues to fix.

1021
00:58:18,080 --> 00:58:21,890
But overall, I can see why we've installed it and the potential benefits.

1022
00:58:21,910 --> 00:58:26,380
It's about critical analysis and constructive feedback, not just venting personal feelings.

1023
00:58:26,660 --> 00:58:27,260
Exactly.

1024
00:58:27,460 --> 00:58:29,020
Professionalism is key here.

1025
00:58:29,800 --> 00:58:35,030
Other examples of questions in this more formal vein include, what are your thoughts on the presentation?

1026
00:58:35,630 --> 00:58:40,280
This asks for a professional opinion on a delivered presentation, maybe after a board meeting.

1027
00:58:41,040 --> 00:58:43,640
Or, what are your thoughts on the new software deployment?

1028
00:58:43,960 --> 00:58:49,270
And very generally and formally, particularly in an email or a very formal meeting, what are your thoughts on the matter?

1029
00:58:49,450 --> 00:58:50,050
Okay.

1030
00:58:50,070 --> 00:58:53,340
So for replies, if someone asks, what are your thoughts on the new project?

1031
00:58:54,280 --> 00:59:01,330
Well, you could respond, well, I can understand the benefits on paper, but I still think there are a lot of details we need to work out before launch.

1032
00:59:01,350 --> 00:59:05,250
This acknowledges positives while clearly identifying challenges that need addressing.

1033
00:59:05,280 --> 00:59:14,860
Or for a positive professional endorsement, personally, I think the project sounds quite promising and we should probably explore it a little further, maybe with a pilot program.

1034
00:59:16,100 --> 00:59:28,370
And if you need more time to think deeply, which is perfectly fine, you can say, overall, I feel that we're going in the right direction, but I'd like to get back to you with some more specific thoughts or ideas after I've had more time to review the data.

1035
00:59:29,130 --> 00:59:29,780
That's a good one.

1036
00:59:30,430 --> 00:59:32,440
It buys you time, but shows you're engaged.

1037
00:59:32,430 --> 00:59:33,220
Exactly.

1038
00:59:33,260 --> 00:59:40,520
It allows you to give a quick summary while asking for time to collect more specific thoughts, showing thoroughness and thoughtfulness.

1039
00:59:40,820 --> 00:59:45,610
And what if you disagree with a proposal but want to be professional and constructive?

1040
00:59:46,370 --> 00:59:55,700
You could state, in my opinion, I don't see this working for us in the end, given our current resources, but I would certainly like to discuss this further and perhaps offer some alternative solutions.

1041
00:59:56,620 --> 00:59:58,660
This expresses disagreement professionally.

1042
00:59:59,010 --> 01:00:04,250
but crucially signals your willingness for a continued, detailed, and productive conversation.

1043
01:00:04,610 --> 01:00:14,370
What's truly fascinating here, I think, is how the language subtly shifts and becomes, you know, a bit more serious and professional with this type of question compared to, what do you think about?

1044
01:00:16,020 --> 01:00:23,440
Understanding this distinction is vital for you to tailor your communication to the formality of the situation and the depth of response expected.

1045
01:00:23,460 --> 01:00:29,610
It ensures you always sound appropriate, thoughtful, and articulate in your workplace English.

1046
01:00:30,490 --> 01:00:33,240
Okay, let's move on to getting news and updates.

1047
01:00:33,440 --> 01:00:38,680
This is often a slightly more casual scenario, perhaps while you're in the break room making coffee or maybe heading out for lunch together.

1048
01:00:39,360 --> 01:00:42,810
Our next question set is, did you hear about the new regulations?

1049
01:00:43,130 --> 01:00:45,090
The main structures are, did you hear about something?

1050
01:00:45,110 --> 01:00:46,090
Did you read about something?

1051
01:00:46,770 --> 01:00:47,690
And did you see something?

1052
01:00:47,930 --> 01:00:51,180
Yeah, this question is brilliant because it's often actually twofold.

1053
01:00:51,780 --> 01:00:57,790
You're literally asking if they've seen or heard the news, perhaps a new company-wide email update that went out to everyone.

1054
01:00:58,270 --> 01:01:01,470
But you're also implicitly asking, so what do you think about them?

1055
01:01:01,600 --> 01:01:02,480
Ah, okay.

1056
01:01:02,920 --> 01:01:03,680
Two questions in one.

1057
01:01:04,120 --> 01:01:04,610
Exactly.

1058
01:01:04,870 --> 01:01:14,160
It's a great way to gauge reactions to recent office developments and engage in that kind of small business English talk about current events or policy changes within the company.

1059
01:01:14,280 --> 01:01:17,000
And there's a crucial nuance with the verbs here, right?

1060
01:01:17,340 --> 01:01:19,200
Depending on how the information was communicated.

1061
01:01:19,500 --> 01:01:25,170
For general news, whether you heard it spoken or saw it written, you use, did you hear about something?

1062
01:01:25,590 --> 01:01:29,310
Like, did you hear about the new regulations from compliance?

1063
01:01:29,770 --> 01:01:39,170
But for information that you know or assume came from text or documents, maybe an email, a report, a memo, it's better to use, did you read about something?

1064
01:01:39,590 --> 01:01:42,490
So, did you read about the layoffs in the memo from HR?

1065
01:01:42,550 --> 01:01:46,140
Or did you read about the new schedule that was posted online?

1066
01:01:46,200 --> 01:01:46,760
Okay, got it.

1067
01:01:46,960 --> 01:01:48,910
Hear about, read about, what about C?

1068
01:01:49,320 --> 01:01:49,590
Right.

1069
01:01:49,610 --> 01:01:56,470
For something visually observed, like a physical memo on a bulletin board or maybe a message that popped up on your screen, it's, did you see something?

1070
01:01:56,870 --> 01:02:00,000
And the important point here is no preposition after see.

1071
01:02:00,560 --> 01:02:01,840
Ah, good distinction.

1072
01:02:02,080 --> 01:02:02,380
Yeah.

1073
01:02:02,760 --> 01:02:06,140
So did you see the new regulations posted by the coffee machine?

1074
01:02:06,540 --> 01:02:10,410
Or did you see that message from the boss that flashed on everyone's computer?

1075
01:02:10,430 --> 01:02:10,690
Okay.

1076
01:02:10,710 --> 01:02:14,590
So did you see that memo, not see about?

1077
01:02:14,690 --> 01:02:15,330
Exactly.

1078
01:02:15,370 --> 01:02:19,100
Or you can say, did you read about that message from the boss?

1079
01:02:20,040 --> 01:02:28,110
The choice of verb really depends on how the information was likely consumed, and using the right one helps you sound more precise and natural.

1080
01:02:28,140 --> 01:02:31,480
So if someone asks you, did you hear about the new regulations?

1081
01:02:32,140 --> 01:02:34,230
A short answer could just be, yeah, I know.

1082
01:02:34,270 --> 01:02:37,830
Or if it was something you had to read, like, did you read that message from the boss?

1083
01:02:38,650 --> 01:02:39,640
And maybe you were busy.

1084
01:02:39,680 --> 01:02:41,540
You could say, yeah, I skimmed through it.

1085
01:02:42,160 --> 01:02:46,490
To skim means to read quickly, just for the general idea, to get the gist.

1086
01:02:46,650 --> 01:02:53,320
Because let's be honest, we're often very busy at work and only have time to skim important but maybe lengthy documents.

1087
01:02:53,360 --> 01:02:54,080
True.

1088
01:02:54,060 --> 01:02:58,980
Similarly, I glanced at it quickly means you just looked very quickly, a brief look.

1089
01:02:59,360 --> 01:03:01,890
These are common and natural replies when you're pressed for time.

1090
01:03:02,030 --> 01:03:04,150
And if you genuinely haven't heard or seen it yet?

1091
01:03:04,170 --> 01:03:07,310
You can simply say, no, I haven't yet or no, I didn't.

1092
01:03:07,720 --> 01:03:11,860
And if you missed it completely and want more information, a good follow up is no, what's happening?

1093
01:03:11,920 --> 01:03:12,600
Can you fill me in?

1094
01:03:13,440 --> 01:03:15,430
That invites your colleague to explain further.

1095
01:03:15,870 --> 01:03:20,670
Or if you did hear about it and maybe know the source, you can provide context.

1096
01:03:21,070 --> 01:03:21,640
Yeah, I did.

1097
01:03:21,800 --> 01:03:23,560
HR told me about it last week in the team meeting.

1098
01:03:24,080 --> 01:03:26,920
This confirms your awareness and gives the source of information.

1099
01:03:27,840 --> 01:03:29,740
And you mentioned a subtle language pattern.

1100
01:03:30,550 --> 01:03:31,470
Yes, it's interesting.

1101
01:03:31,490 --> 01:03:35,710
You might say, well, I read the email pretty quickly, but I need to read more about the paperwork.

1102
01:03:36,330 --> 01:03:37,130
Notice the difference.

1103
01:03:37,810 --> 01:03:43,620
You read an email, but you read more about a topic when you want to learn additional, deeper information.

1104
01:03:44,140 --> 01:03:45,460
It's subtle, but common.

1105
01:03:45,740 --> 01:03:47,230
Interesting.

1106
01:03:47,250 --> 01:03:53,060
And what about for closer colleagues if you're comfortable expressing stronger feelings in a more informal setting?

1107
01:03:53,080 --> 01:03:53,200
Right.

1108
01:03:53,220 --> 01:03:54,480
You might say, yeah, I saw it.

1109
01:03:54,500 --> 01:03:55,120
I can't believe it.

1110
01:03:55,240 --> 01:03:57,490
Or maybe more negatively, yeah, I saw it.

1111
01:03:57,510 --> 01:03:57,930
I hate it.

1112
01:03:57,970 --> 01:03:59,970
It's going to make our workflow so much more complicated.

1113
01:04:00,050 --> 01:04:00,310
Okay.

1114
01:04:00,360 --> 01:04:01,580
That sounds a bit like a cheeky.

1115
01:04:01,560 --> 01:04:03,500
Yes, that last one.

1116
01:04:04,060 --> 01:04:18,800
If you're looking to share strong opinions or perhaps engage in a bit of office gossip, meaning sharing opinions informally with trusted colleagues, that's the phrase for you, it definitely shows a level of comfort and shared understanding or maybe shared frustration with your colleagues.

1117
01:04:19,100 --> 01:04:30,990
So this question structure is really invaluable for you, the listener, to stay informed about office news, gauge your colleagues' reactions, and engage in the more social, informal side of office life.

1118
01:04:31,090 --> 01:04:35,130
All while being mindful, of course, of how much you share and with whom.

1119
01:04:35,780 --> 01:04:38,320
It's key for building that informal rapport.

1120
01:04:38,500 --> 01:04:39,060
Definitely.

1121
01:04:39,540 --> 01:04:39,760
Okay.

1122
01:04:39,780 --> 01:04:46,170
We've covered a huge range of ways to confidently start conversations, really setting the stage for good workplace interaction.

1123
01:04:47,090 --> 01:04:52,540
But now for the flip side, which honestly, many people find equally, if not more, challenging.

1124
01:04:53,300 --> 01:04:59,490
How do you gracefully end a conversation politely and professionally without hurting anyone's feelings or appearing rude?

1125
01:04:59,690 --> 01:05:00,470
Hmm.

1126
01:05:00,490 --> 01:05:00,830
Yeah.

1127
01:05:01,090 --> 01:05:02,540
This is where a lot of people struggle, isn't it?

1128
01:05:02,560 --> 01:05:03,680
They feel kind of trapped.

1129
01:05:03,820 --> 01:05:04,140
Totally.

1130
01:05:04,400 --> 01:05:12,010
The goal here is to give you some example sentences that let you, you know, walk away and get back to your tasks while subtly signaling that you do want to talk again later.

1131
01:05:12,410 --> 01:05:14,190
You're not cutting off the conversation forever.

1132
01:05:14,610 --> 01:05:19,020
You're indicating a desire for future connection and basically maintaining the relationship.

1133
01:05:19,040 --> 01:05:21,710
It's about being efficient while remaining friendly.

1134
01:05:22,130 --> 01:05:22,570
Okay.

1135
01:05:22,590 --> 01:05:26,270
Let's start with some British English phrases for ending conversations.

1136
01:05:27,050 --> 01:05:36,960
My first one often starts with a very, very British verbal cue that almost every British person says before they stand up or before they leave.

1137
01:05:36,980 --> 01:05:37,580
Right.

1138
01:05:38,220 --> 01:05:38,480
Yes.

1139
01:05:38,620 --> 01:05:39,000
They're right.

1140
01:05:39,040 --> 01:05:39,910
It's incredibly common.

1141
01:05:40,250 --> 01:05:42,830
And it often comes with a subtle physical cue, doesn't it?

1142
01:05:42,870 --> 01:05:51,700
Like a light slap on the thighs if you're sitting down or a quick, almost imperceptible glance at your watch or phone, maybe while holding a cup of tea.

1143
01:05:52,160 --> 01:05:55,130
It's like a nonverbal signal that you're preparing to transition.

1144
01:05:55,210 --> 01:05:55,850
Exactly.

1145
01:05:55,970 --> 01:05:59,100
So after a conversation, you might say, right, I should probably get back to my desk.

1146
01:05:59,120 --> 01:06:00,220
I'll chat to you in a bit.

1147
01:06:00,240 --> 01:06:02,060
And that I'll chat to you in a bit is key.

1148
01:06:02,440 --> 01:06:09,280
It explicitly signals future interaction, making you sound friendly and keen to connect again soon rather than just dismissive.

1149
01:06:09,800 --> 01:06:11,500
It leaves the door open, as you said.

1150
01:06:11,540 --> 01:06:13,050
Another common one.

1151
01:06:13,110 --> 01:06:14,630
OK, I've really got to get on with that email.

1152
01:06:14,810 --> 01:06:15,430
Catch up later.

1153
01:06:15,550 --> 01:06:20,830
Yeah, get on with means to start or continue working diligently on something, like a pressing email.

1154
01:06:21,720 --> 01:06:29,450
And catch up later, again, indicates a desire for a future conversation, showing you value the connection even though you have to leave now.

1155
01:06:29,850 --> 01:06:34,660
And if you have a genuine time constraint, which is always a polite and understandable reason.

1156
01:06:35,500 --> 01:06:36,880
Sorry, I've got a meeting in a few minutes.

1157
01:06:36,900 --> 01:06:37,840
Let's pick this up later.

1158
01:06:37,950 --> 01:06:46,330
Providing a clear, time-sensitive reason for leaving is always polite, and let's pick this up later, is a direct phrase for resuming the discussion at another time.

1159
01:06:46,950 --> 01:06:51,290
It implies, this conversation is important, but time is short right now.

1160
01:06:51,970 --> 01:07:02,530
So the consistent element across all these British phrases seems to be a clear, polite statement about needing to leave for a specific reason, followed by a phrase that expresses a desire for future conversation.

1161
01:07:02,610 --> 01:07:03,210
Exactly.

1162
01:07:03,270 --> 01:07:07,220
This helps you sound friendly, engaged, and professional, not dismissive or rude.

1163
01:07:07,800 --> 01:07:11,700
It's all about managing expectations and maintaining those relationships.

1164
01:07:11,720 --> 01:07:20,090
You know, for some American English phrases for ending conversations, these can sometimes be a bit more direct or rely on different common idioms, right?

1165
01:07:20,110 --> 01:07:21,310
Yeah, sometimes.

1166
01:07:22,090 --> 01:07:25,680
My personal favorite and a real go-to is, Oh, look at the time.

1167
01:07:25,720 --> 01:07:26,840
I've got to hop into a meeting.

1168
01:07:26,860 --> 01:07:28,340
Let's touch base tomorrow.

1169
01:07:28,620 --> 01:07:29,220
Classic.

1170
01:07:29,200 --> 01:07:34,830
It is, oh, look at the time, is a classic universally understood and acceptable excuse.

1171
01:07:35,250 --> 01:07:36,350
I've got to hop into a meeting.

1172
01:07:36,370 --> 01:07:42,540
Or you might hear jump into a meeting means you need to enter or start a meeting immediately, often implying a quick departure.

1173
01:07:43,040 --> 01:07:49,870
And let's touch base tomorrow means let's contact each other tomorrow, signaling a clear intention to continue the conversation or topic.

1174
01:07:49,950 --> 01:07:52,140
It's a very, very common business idiom.

1175
01:07:52,240 --> 01:07:54,100
That's very common and really efficient.

1176
01:07:54,340 --> 01:07:56,140
What's another useful one for those busy moments?

1177
01:07:56,120 --> 01:07:58,820
Uh, how about, okay, I've got to tie things up here.

1178
01:07:58,860 --> 01:08:00,180
Let's keep in touch about the results.

1179
01:08:00,830 --> 01:08:07,090
Tie things up means you need to bring tasks or maybe a situation to a close or finish something indicating you have work to complete.

1180
01:08:07,860 --> 01:08:08,060
Okay.

1181
01:08:08,080 --> 01:08:12,420
And let's keep in touch is another simple, easy way to indicate ongoing communication.

1182
01:08:12,960 --> 01:08:21,330
This phrase can sound maybe a touch more direct and it's particularly useful if you are in a rush or maybe if you're in a supervisory role needing to end the chat.

1183
01:08:21,470 --> 01:08:25,470
And a simple direct one that you can use in almost any situation.

1184
01:08:26,030 --> 01:08:26,830
You should probably get back.

1185
01:08:27,210 --> 01:08:30,580
You can add specifics like to work, to my office, or to the meeting.

1186
01:08:30,620 --> 01:08:30,840
Yeah.

1187
01:08:30,860 --> 01:08:33,060
And then, of course, the classic polite closure.

1188
01:08:33,320 --> 01:08:33,880
See you later.

1189
01:08:33,920 --> 01:08:36,360
Short, sweet, and effective.

1190
01:08:36,380 --> 01:08:36,940
Absolutely.

1191
01:08:37,000 --> 01:08:40,310
And here's one of my ultimate favorite office cliches.

1192
01:08:40,570 --> 01:08:46,690
But it comes with a really crucial nuance for you, the listener, that you absolutely need to be aware of.

1193
01:08:48,030 --> 01:08:49,940
Let's circle back to this at a later time.

1194
01:08:50,100 --> 01:08:51,780
Ah, circle back.

1195
01:08:51,940 --> 01:08:52,990
I hear that one a lot.

1196
01:08:53,030 --> 01:08:53,390
You do.

1197
01:08:53,650 --> 01:09:16,450
Now, while it can genuinely mean, we'll discuss this later, and it's often used that way in project management, it's really important to know that often, maybe even a lot of the times, especially if it comes from someone in a higher position or someone who's maybe tired of a topic, if they say, let's circle back to that, it might actually be a polite way to say, stop talking about that for now, or I'm done with this topic, let's move on.

1198
01:09:16,710 --> 01:09:17,510
Wow, okay.

1199
01:09:17,490 --> 01:09:21,900
It depends heavily on the situation, their tone, and the context of the conversation.

1200
01:09:21,940 --> 01:09:29,290
This is a very important aha moment for recognizing unspoken social cues in workplace conversations.

1201
01:09:29,810 --> 01:09:31,190
It can be professional deflection.

1202
01:09:31,670 --> 01:09:33,530
You'll definitely hear it differently now, won't you?

1203
01:09:33,650 --> 01:09:34,380
I definitely will.

1204
